declare d jsonb := $fa$[{"slug":"introduction-to-functional-health","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_introduction_to_functional_health","title":"Introduction to functional health","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 1","summary":"Functional health is how well your body works for you day to day, and over the next 14 days we build it from where you are, one small step at a time.","body":"Most of us only think about health when something goes wrong. But health is also the ordinary things: waking up with energy, a meal that sits well, a calm head in the afternoon, a body that moves without complaint, sleep that actually rests you. That is what we mean by functional health, and it is what this track is about.\n\n## What functional health is\n\nFunctional health looks at how your body is working, not only at whether a test comes back normal. Two people can have the same results on paper and feel completely different in daily life. One sleeps well and has steady energy; the other is tired by mid-afternoon, bloated after meals and wired at night.\n\nYour body is one system. What you eat affects your energy and your sleep. How you sleep affects your hunger and your mood. Stress shows up in your digestion. Movement helps almost everything. So instead of treating each problem on its own, we look at the patterns that connect them, and we work on the foundations underneath.\n\n## The four pillars\n\nEverything in this track rests on four pillars. You will meet one each day this first week.\n\n1. **Nutrition** (day 2). Not a diet. How you eat matters as much as what you eat: slowly, without a screen, with real food on the plate.\n2. **Movement** (day 3). Not sport. The everyday capacity to get up, bend, carry and climb. Some activity is better than none, and every bit counts.\n3. **Sleep** (day 4). The time your body uses to recover. Sleep experts recommend that most adults sleep seven hours or more on a regular basis. Light, caffeine and your evening all shape how well you sleep.\n4. **Mental and emotional health** (day 5). Breathing, attention and connection. Your mood is not separate from your body; research links movement, sleep and other daily habits with mental well-being.\n\nThe pillars support each other. A walk after lunch helps your energy in the afternoon; a calmer evening helps your sleep; better sleep makes the next day's choices easier.\n\n## Why we start from where you are\n\nThere is no ideal starting point. A plan built for an imaginary person rarely fits a real one. So before we change much, we want to see your real pattern: what you eat, how your days feel, how you sleep.\n\nThat is why the first two habits are simply about noticing. Studies of goal-setting find that keeping track of your progress makes you more likely to reach your goals, especially when you actually record it. Tracking is not about control, and it should never make you obsessive. It should make you clearer. One bad day tells us little; a trend over two weeks tells us a lot.\n\n## How the 14 days work\n\n- **One short step a day.** Each day brings a theme and, most days, one or two new habits. They are small on purpose: a few minutes, never a new hour in your diary.\n- **Your evening check-in.** One minute to rate how the day landed. After three days in a row, your curves appear in Trends. See *Your functional check-in, explained*.\n- **Your meal photos.** A quick photo before the first bite. No counting. See *What your meal photos tell us*.\n- **Two pauses.** Day 6 looks back on your first week. Day 13 is where you choose the habits you keep.\n- **Your review.** On day 14 we look at your two weeks together, with Thomas.\n\nIf you miss a day, simply carry on the next one. Consistency beats perfection.\n\nIf you have a medical condition, are pregnant or take regular medication, ask your doctor or your nutritionist before changing your routine.\n\n## What to do\n\nToday, two habits.\n\n**Log a meal**\n- Open Log a meal, point, shoot.\n- Before the first bite, not after.\n- Drinks and snacks count.\n\n**Do your functional check-in**\n- Five sliders: energy, sleep, mood, calmness, digestion.\n- Around the same time each evening.\n- Honest beats perfect.\n\n## What to look for\n\nOver the next few days, many people notice that:\n\n- they start to see their real pattern, without counting;\n- they notice what keeps them full;\n- their own patterns begin to show, and their curves appear in Trends.\n\n**Functional health starts with seeing clearly where you are; small steps, repeated, do the rest.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Bull FC, et al. World Health Organization 2020 guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviour. *Br J Sports Med.* 2020;54(24):1451-1462. PMID: 33239350. [doi:10.1136/bjsports-2020-102955](https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2020-102955)\n2. Watson NF, et al. Recommended Amount of Sleep for a Healthy Adult: A Joint Consensus Statement of the American Academy of Sleep Medicine and Sleep Research Society. *Sleep.* 2015;38(6):843-844. PMID: 26039963. [doi:10.5665/sleep.4716](https://doi.org/10.5665/sleep.4716)\n3. Firth J, et al. A meta-review of \"lifestyle psychiatry\": the role of exercise, smoking, diet and sleep in the prevention and treatment of mental disorders. *World Psychiatry.* 2020;19(3):360-380. PMID: 32931092. [doi:10.1002/wps.20773](https://doi.org/10.1002/wps.20773)\n4. Harkin B, et al. Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta-analysis of the experimental evidence. *Psychol Bull.* 2016;142(2):198-229. PMID: 26479070. [doi:10.1037/bul0000025](https://doi.org/10.1037/bul0000025)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4"]},{"slug":"what-your-meal-photos-tell-us","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_what_your_meal_photos_tell_us","title":"What your meal photos tell us","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 1","summary":"A quick photo before the first bite shows us your real eating pattern, with no counting and no weighing.","body":"Most of us don't really know what we eat. Not because we hide it, but because memory is kind to us. The handful of nuts, the second coffee, the biscuit at four o'clock: they disappear by the evening. A photo doesn't forget.\n\n## Why photos, not counting\n\nCounting calories or weighing food takes time, and for many people it turns eating into a test. That is not what we want. A photo takes two seconds, and it keeps the meal as it really was.\n\nResearch on food records shows that adding pictures to a food diary helps people report what they eat more completely; people tend to under-report less when they snap their meals. And studies of people changing their eating habits keep finding the same thing: those who keep a record of what they eat tend to do better, although the quality of that evidence is modest.\n\n## What we look at\n\nWe are not judging your plate. We look for patterns over days, such as:\n\n- **Colour and variety.** How many different plants appear across the week.\n- **Protein.** Whether each meal has a source of it.\n- **The kind of carbohydrate.** Whole grains, beans and fruit, or mostly white bread, pastries and sugary drinks.\n- **Rhythm.** When you eat, how long the gaps are, whether meals get skipped.\n- **The in-betweens.** Drinks, snacks and grazing, which often tell us the most.\n\nPut next to your evening check-in, this helps us see what keeps you full and steady, and what leaves you tired or bloated.\n\n## How to snap fast\n\n- **Before the first bite.** Afterwards the plate is half-eaten, and it is easy to forget.\n- **The whole plate, from above,** in reasonable light. It doesn't need to look good.\n- **Drinks and snacks too.** A coffee, a glass of juice, an apple: they all count.\n- **Missed one?** No problem. Carry on with the next meal.\n\n## What to do\n\n**Log a meal**\n- Open Log a meal, point, shoot.\n- Before the first bite, not after.\n- Drinks and snacks count.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice that:\n\n- they see their real pattern, without counting;\n- their nutritionist sees what they eat, so advice fits their real life;\n- they start to notice what keeps them full.\n\n**A photo before the first bite is the easiest honest food diary there is.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Boushey CJ, et al. New mobile methods for dietary assessment: review of image-assisted and image-based dietary assessment methods. *Proc Nutr Soc.* 2017;76(3):283-294. PMID: 27938425. [doi:10.1017/S0029665116002913](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0029665116002913)\n2. Burke LE, et al. Self-monitoring in weight loss: a systematic review of the literature. *J Am Diet Assoc.* 2011;111(1):92-102. PMID: 21185970. [doi:10.1016/j.jada.2010.10.008](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jada.2010.10.008)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:3","member/quick"]},{"slug":"your-functional-check-in-explained","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_your_functional_check_in_explained","title":"Your functional check-in, explained","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 1","summary":"One minute each evening to rate five markers turns how your days feel into curves you can actually see.","body":"Your body doesn't respond the same way every day. The same lunch, the same walk, the same night can feel completely different depending on stress, sleep and what came before. Without feedback, a plan is a theory. With feedback, it becomes yours.\n\n## The five markers\n\nEach evening you move five sliders:\n\n1. **Energy.** How much you had today, and whether it held up.\n2. **Sleep.** How well you slept last night, and how rested you felt.\n3. **Mood.** Your general state of mind: flat, irritable, steady or bright.\n4. **Calmness.** How settled or tense you felt through the day.\n5. **Digestion.** Comfort after meals: lightness, bloating, heaviness.\n\nThe question behind each one is simple: did today give you energy, cost you energy, or keep things steady?\n\n## Why the evening, and why every day\n\nRating the day while it is still fresh is more accurate than trying to remember a whole week later. Researchers who study daily life use this same idea: short, repeated ratings close to the moment reduce the blur of memory.\n\nDoing it around the same time each evening makes the ratings comparable, and it helps the check-in become a habit rather than a task. It takes about a minute.\n\nAnd being honest matters more than being positive. A low score is not a failure; it is information. It is often the most useful entry of the week.\n\n## How your curves become your Trends\n\nAfter three days in a row, your curves appear in Trends. From there, the picture grows each evening.\n\nLook for trends, not single days. One poor night tells us little. A week of steadier energy since you started walking after lunch tells us a lot. Over the 14 days, your curves show which habits make a difference for you, and they give us something concrete to look at together at your review.\n\nTracking is meant to make you clearer, not anxious. If it ever starts to feel like a chore, keep it short and simply carry on.\n\n## What to do\n\n**Do your functional check-in**\n- Five sliders: energy, sleep, mood, calmness, digestion.\n- Around the same time each evening.\n- Honest beats perfect.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice that:\n\n- they start to see their own patterns;\n- their curves appear in Trends;\n- over time, it shows what their habits change.\n\n**One honest minute each evening turns how you feel into something you can see, and act on.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Harkin B, et al. Does monitoring goal progress promote goal attainment? A meta-analysis of the experimental evidence. *Psychol Bull.* 2016;142(2):198-229. PMID: 26479070. [doi:10.1037/bul0000025](https://doi.org/10.1037/bul0000025)\n2. Shiffman S, et al. Ecological momentary assessment. *Annu Rev Clin Psychol.* 2008;4:1-32. PMID: 18509902. [doi:10.1146/annurev.clinpsy.3.022806.091415](https://doi.org/10.1146/annurev.clinpsy.3.022806.091415)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:3","member/quick"]},{"slug":"mindful-eating","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_mindful_eating","title":"Mindful eating","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 2","summary":"Eating without your phone, chewing well and taking a few breaths changes how you digest, how full you feel and how much you want later.","body":"Think of your last lunch. Where were you? Many of us eat at the desk, in front of a screen, or standing up between two tasks. The meal is gone in a few minutes and we barely remember it. Then, an hour later, we are hungry again, or heavy and bloated.\n\nNutrition is not only what is on your plate. It is also how you eat it. That is where we start the nutrition pillar.\n\n## Why the phone matters\n\nWhen your attention is on a screen, your brain registers the meal less well. A review of experiments on attention and eating found that people who ate while distracted ate more at that meal, and even more at the next one. When people were helped to remember what they had eaten, they tended to eat less later.\n\nIn other words, a meal you didn't notice doesn't count as much for your brain. You finish it, but you don't really feel you've eaten. Putting the phone away is the simplest way to let the meal register.\n\n## Why chewing matters\n\nDigestion starts in your mouth. Chewing breaks food into smaller pieces and mixes it with saliva, which already begins to digest starches. Food that is chewed well is easier for your stomach and gut to handle.\n\nChewing also slows you down. Fullness signals take time to travel from your gut to your brain, and when you eat fast, you can finish before they arrive. A review of studies on chewing found that chewing more tended to reduce hunger and how much people ate, possibly through the gut hormones linked to fullness. The evidence is still early, but the direction is consistent.\n\nA simple trick: put your fork down between bites. It gives you a natural pause and makes chewing well almost automatic.\n\n## Why a few breaths, and a glass of water\n\nYour nervous system has two broad modes: one for action and stress, one for rest and digestion. If you sit down to eat straight from a stressful meeting, your body is still in action mode. Three slow breaths before you start are a small signal to shift gear. Many people find that the meal feels calmer and sits better afterwards.\n\nA glass of water with the meal is simply an easy way to drink enough through the day. Taking a sip between bites also slows the pace of the meal a little, which brings you back to chewing.\n\n## Notice when you are full\n\nMindful eating is not a set of rules. It is paying attention: to the taste, to the texture, and to the moment you feel satisfied. Reviews of mindful eating suggest it works mainly by helping people listen to their internal signals of hunger and fullness, rather than to outside cues such as the size of the plate or the clock. The results are mixed across studies, but the approach is low-risk and many people find it helps.\n\nHalfway through your meal, pause and ask: how hungry am I now? There is no right answer. You are just learning to hear your body again.\n\nIf you have, or have had, a difficult relationship with food, go gently, and talk to your nutritionist about what feels right for you.\n\n## What to do\n\n**Mindful eating: no phone, chew, breathe, a glass of water**\n- Phone away, screen off.\n- Chew each bite well; put the fork down between bites.\n- Three breaths before you start, a glass of water with the meal.\n\nStart with one meal a day. Lunch is often the easiest.\n\n## What to look for\n\nOver the next days, many people notice:\n\n- lighter digestion, less bloating;\n- feeling full sooner, with fewer cravings later;\n- calmer meals.\n\n**A meal you actually notice satisfies you more: phone away, chew slowly, breathe.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Robinson E, et al. Eating attentively: a systematic review and meta-analysis of the effect of food intake memory and awareness on eating. *Am J Clin Nutr.* 2013;97(4):728-742. PMID: 23446890. [doi:10.3945/ajcn.112.045245](https://doi.org/10.3945/ajcn.112.045245)\n2. Miquel-Kergoat S, et al. Effects of chewing on appetite, food intake and gut hormones: A systematic review and meta-analysis. *Physiol Behav.* 2015;151:88-96. PMID: 26188140. [doi:10.1016/j.physbeh.2015.07.017](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.physbeh.2015.07.017)\n3. Warren JM, et al. A structured literature review on the role of mindfulness, mindful eating and intuitive eating in changing eating behaviours: effectiveness and associated potential mechanisms. *Nutr Res Rev.* 2017;30(2):272-283. PMID: 28718396. [doi:10.1017/S0954422417000154](https://doi.org/10.1017/S0954422417000154)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/nutrition"]},{"slug":"the-5-minute-walk-after-eating","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_the_5_minute_walk_after_eating","title":"The 5-minute walk after eating","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 2","summary":"A few easy minutes on your feet after a meal help your muscles use the energy you have just eaten, and can soften the afternoon dip.","body":"You know the feeling: lunch is over, you sit back down, and an hour later your eyelids are heavy. A short walk after eating is one of the simplest ways to change that. No kit, no sweat, five minutes.\n\n## What happens after a meal\n\nWhen you eat, the carbohydrates in your meal are broken down into glucose (sugar) that enters your blood. That is normal and useful: it is fuel. Your body then works to move it out of the blood and into your cells.\n\nYour muscles are the biggest user of that glucose. When they work, even gently, they take up more of it. Think of them as a sponge: the meal brings the fuel, and the walk switches on the tissue that can use it.\n\nThis is not about fearing carbohydrates. It is simply about helping your body handle a meal more smoothly.\n\n## What the research shows\n\nStudies that break up long periods of sitting with short bouts of light walking find lower blood sugar and insulin after meals than when people stay seated, and walking does better than simply standing. In one trial in people with type 2 diabetes, walking for 10 minutes after each main meal lowered blood sugar after meals more than one 30-minute walk at another time of day. The effect was largest after the evening meal.\n\nFor digestion, the evidence is smaller. One study in healthy volunteers found that a walk after a meal helped the stomach empty faster. Many people also find they feel less heavy, but your own feedback matters most here.\n\nFive minutes is our starting dose. Some of the studies used longer walks, so think of today as the first step; on day 8 it grows, in *The walk after every meal*.\n\n## Fitting it in, even at work\n\n- **Right after eating, or within half an hour.** Whatever is comfortable.\n- **Outside if you can.** Daylight and fresh air are a bonus.\n- **No outside? The corridor works too.** Take the stairs, walk to the far coffee machine, take a call on your feet.\n- **Easy pace.** You should be able to chat. This is not a workout.\n\nIf you have a medical condition, check with your doctor before changing your routine. If walking causes discomfort, slow down or wait a little longer after the meal.\n\n## What to do\n\n**A 5-minute walk after eating**\n- Right after the meal, or within half an hour.\n- Outside if you can; the corridor works too.\n- Easy pace, no sweat.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- steadier energy after meals;\n- easier digestion;\n- less of the afternoon dip.\n\n**Five easy minutes on your feet after eating help your body make good use of the meal.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Buffey AJ, et al. The Acute Effects of Interrupting Prolonged Sitting Time in Adults with Standing and Light-Intensity Walking on Biomarkers of Cardiometabolic Health in Adults: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis. *Sports Med.* 2022;52(8):1765-1787. PMID: 35147898. [doi:10.1007/s40279-022-01649-4](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40279-022-01649-4)\n2. Reynolds AN, et al. Advice to walk after meals is more effective for lowering postprandial glycaemia in type 2 diabetes mellitus than advice that does not specify timing: a randomised crossover study. *Diabetologia.* 2016;59(12):2572-2578. PMID: 27747394. [doi:10.1007/s00125-016-4085-2](https://doi.org/10.1007/s00125-016-4085-2)\n3. Franke A, et al. Postprandial walking but not consumption of alcoholic digestifs or espresso accelerates gastric emptying in healthy volunteers. *J Gastrointestin Liver Dis.* 2008;17(1):27-31. PMID: 18392240.","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:3","member/quick","pillar/nutrition"]},{"slug":"exercise-snacks-foundation","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_exercise_snacks_foundation","title":"Exercise snacks: small signals that add up","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 3","summary":"An exercise snack is a short burst of effort, such as 10 squats or a minute up the stairs, and several of them across the day add up.","body":"A workout doesn't have to be one long block. Your body responds to the movement you accumulate over the day and the week, not only to what happens in a gym. An exercise snack is the smallest useful piece of that.\n\n## What an exercise snack is\n\nResearchers describe exercise snacks as short bursts of effort, often a minute or less, done a few times across the day. Not random movement, but a small, deliberate dose:\n\n- 10 squats, up from a chair and back down;\n- a minute up the stairs, at a pace that gets you breathing;\n- 10 push-ups against a wall.\n\nThe best time is after you have been sitting a while. It breaks the sitting, and it wakes you up.\n\n## Different snacks, different signals\n\nNot every snack does the same thing.\n\n- **Heart and breath snacks**, like stairs or a brisk uphill walk, mainly challenge your heart, lungs and circulation.\n- **Strength snacks**, like squats or wall push-ups, mainly ask your muscles to work against resistance.\n\nBoth are useful. Pick the one that suits the moment.\n\n## Why short bursts add up\n\nThe evidence is young but encouraging. Reviews of the trials so far find exercise snacks are feasible and appear safe for adults, including older adults, and can improve fitness, especially in people who were inactive. In a large study using activity trackers, people who did no formal exercise but had a few short, vigorous bursts in their daily life, such as climbing stairs or walking briskly, had a lower risk of dying over the following years. That kind of study shows a link, not proof of cause, and the researchers are still testing how far the benefits go.\n\nWhat it does show: short does not mean meaningless.\n\n## What to do\n\n**Optional: an exercise snack**\n- 10 squats, or a minute up the stairs.\n- Or 10 push-ups against a wall.\n- Any time you've been sitting a while.\n\nToday this is optional. On day 7 it becomes *One exercise snack a day*.\n\nGo at your own pace, and stop if it hurts. If you have a heart condition, high blood pressure, joint pain or are pregnant, check with your doctor before adding vigorous bursts.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- clarity of mind;\n- more energy;\n- steadier blood sugar (you won't feel this one directly, and the evidence so far comes from small studies);\n- over time, a stronger body overall.\n\n**Short does not mean meaningless: a focused burst of effort is a real signal, and small signals add up.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Islam H, et al. Exercise Snacks: A Novel Strategy to Improve Cardiometabolic Health. *Exerc Sport Sci Rev.* 2022;50(1):31-37. PMID: 34669625. [doi:10.1249/JES.0000000000000275](https://doi.org/10.1249/JES.0000000000000275)\n2. Jones MD, et al. Exercise Snacks and Other Forms of Intermittent Physical Activity for Improving Health in Adults and Older Adults: A Scoping Review of Epidemiological, Experimental and Qualitative Studies. *Sports Med.* 2024;54(4):813-835. PMID: 38190022. [doi:10.1007/s40279-023-01983-1](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40279-023-01983-1)\n3. Stamatakis E, et al. Association of wearable device-measured vigorous intermittent lifestyle physical activity with mortality. *Nat Med.* 2022;28(12):2521-2529. PMID: 36482104. [doi:10.1038/s41591-022-02100-x](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-022-02100-x)\n4. Wan KW, et al. Effects of Exercise Snacks on Cardiometabolic Health and Body Composition in Adults: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. *Scand J Med Sci Sports.* 2025;35(8):e70114. PMID: 40814152. [doi:10.1111/sms.70114](https://doi.org/10.1111/sms.70114)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:3","member/quick","pillar/mouvement"]},{"slug":"little-and-often","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_little_and_often","title":"Little and often","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 3","summary":"Movement for health is about keeping your body capable, not about sport, and a few minutes you actually do each day beat a big plan you don't keep.","body":"Many of us have started an ambitious exercise plan in January and quietly dropped it by February. The plan wasn't wrong. It was just too big for a real life. Today, for the movement pillar, we go the other way: tiny, daily, easy to keep.\n\n## Functional capacity, not sport\n\nYou don't need to be sporty to move well. What we care about is functional capacity: your body's ability to do what daily life asks of it. Getting up from a chair without your hands. Carrying the shopping up the stairs. Turning your head to reverse the car. Bending down to tie a shoe, and getting back up.\n\nThese abilities fade quietly when we sit most of the day, and they come back with regular, gentle use. Joints like to move through their range. Muscles like to be asked to work. Blood flow and breathing pick up the moment you get going. None of that needs a gym.\n\n## Why small counts\n\nFor a long time, advice said exercise only \"counted\" in blocks of ten minutes or more. That has changed. A review carried out for the US physical activity guidelines found that activity of any bout length was linked to better health, including a longer life. The World Health Organization's 2020 guidelines say it plainly: some physical activity is better than none, and more is better still.\n\nSo your three minutes in the morning are not a token gesture. They are real movement, and they add up across the week.\n\n## Why small sticks\n\nThere is a second reason to start small, and it may matter even more: habits. Research on habit formation shows that behaviours become automatic when they are repeated in the same situation, at the same moment of the day. The behaviour itself doesn't need to be big. What matters is that it is easy enough to do on your worst day.\n\nThree minutes after you get up, two minutes before bed. Short enough that \"I don't have time\" stops being true. Once the habit exists, it is much easier to grow it, and over the coming days we will.\n\n## Two moments: morning and evening\n\n**In the morning**, three minutes to wake the body. Gentle circles of the neck, shoulders and hips loosen what stiffened overnight. Ten slow squats wake up the biggest muscles you have. Reaching up and folding down, with a breath, gets blood moving and gives you a first win before the day starts.\n\n**In the evening**, two minutes of gentle stretching before bed. Slow and calm, with a long breath out each time. This is not about flexibility goals. It is a way to release the day's sitting from your hips, the backs of your legs and your chest, and a clear signal that the day is ending. Many people find it helps them settle for sleep.\n\nMovement should never hurt. Stretch only to a gentle pull, never into pain, and stop if anything hurts. If you have a joint problem, an injury or a heart condition, check with your doctor or physiotherapist first.\n\n## What to do\n\n**3 minutes of movement** (morning)\n- Neck, shoulder and hip circles.\n- 10 slow squats.\n- Reach up, fold down, breathe.\n\n**2 minutes of gentle stretching before bed** (evening)\n- Slow, never into pain.\n- Hamstrings, hips, an open chest.\n- A long breath out with each stretch.\n\nFeeling like more? Read *Exercise snacks: small signals that add up* for today's optional extra.\n\n## What to look for\n\nOver the next days, many people notice:\n\n- less stiffness in the morning, and feeling more awake, sooner;\n- a first win in the day;\n- looser back and hips, and an easier time falling asleep;\n- a clear end to the day.\n\n**Five minutes a day you actually do will take you further than an hour you keep postponing.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Bull FC, et al. World Health Organization 2020 guidelines on physical activity and sedentary behaviour. *Br J Sports Med.* 2020;54(24):1451-1462. PMID: 33239350. [doi:10.1136/bjsports-2020-102955](https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2020-102955)\n2. Jakicic JM, et al. Association between Bout Duration of Physical Activity and Health: Systematic Review. *Med Sci Sports Exerc.* 2019;51(6):1213-1219. PMID: 31095078. [doi:10.1249/MSS.0000000000001933](https://doi.org/10.1249/MSS.0000000000001933)\n3. Gardner B, et al. Making health habitual: the psychology of 'habit-formation' and general practice. *Br J Gen Pract.* 2012;62(605):664-666. PMID: 23211256. [doi:10.3399/bjgp12X659466](https://doi.org/10.3399/bjgp12X659466)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/mouvement"]},{"slug":"caffeine-and-your-sleep","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_caffeine_and_your_sleep","title":"Caffeine and your sleep","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 4","summary":"Caffeine stays in your body for hours, so your last coffee or tea of the day should come before 16:00.","body":"Many people say, \"Coffee doesn't affect my sleep, I fall asleep fine.\" Sometimes that is true. But caffeine can still make sleep lighter and shorter without you noticing. Then you wake up tired, reach for another coffee, and the loop starts again.\n\n## How caffeine works\n\nThrough the day, a substance called adenosine builds up in your brain. The more of it there is, the sleepier you feel. This \"sleep pressure\" is part of what helps you fall asleep at night. Caffeine works by blocking adenosine's effect. You still build up sleep pressure; you just feel it less.\n\nThe catch is that caffeine leaves the body slowly. It takes many hours to clear, and how fast varies a lot from person to person: genes, age, hormones, smoking and some medicines all play a part. So an afternoon coffee can still be working at bedtime.\n\n## What the research shows\n\nIn a well-known trial, a strong dose of caffeine taken six hours before bed still disturbed people's sleep. A 2023 review that pooled 24 studies found caffeine shortened total sleep time and reduced deep sleep, the most restoring stage. From these results, the authors estimate that a usual cup of coffee should come almost nine hours before bed to avoid losing sleep.\n\n16:00 is a practical limit that works for most days. If you go to bed early, or you know you are sensitive to caffeine, earlier is better.\n\n## What counts, and what to drink instead\n\nCaffeine is in more than coffee:\n\n- Coffee, including espresso and iced coffee.\n- Black and green tea, including matcha and iced tea.\n- Cola and energy drinks.\n- Maté, which is often mistaken for a herbal tea.\n- Cocoa and dark chocolate have smaller amounts.\n\nAfter 16:00, choose decaf, a real herbal tea (such as camomile, verbena, peppermint or rooibos) or water.\n\n## What to do\n\nThe habit is **No caffeine after 16:00**.\n\n- Coffee, black and green tea, cola and energy drinks all count.\n- After 16:00: decaf, herbal tea or water.\n- If 16:00 is hard, move it 30 minutes earlier each day.\n\nIf you drink a lot of caffeine, cutting back fast can bring a headache or tiredness for a few days. Going step by step makes it easier.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Falling asleep faster.\n- Deeper sleep.\n- Needing less caffeine the next morning.\n\n**Enjoy your coffee in the morning, and let 16:00 be your last cup.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Drake C, et al. Caffeine effects on sleep taken 0, 3, or 6 hours before going to bed. *Journal of Clinical Sleep Medicine*. 2013. PMID: 24235903. DOI: 10.5664/jcsm.3170\n2. Gardiner C, et al. The effect of caffeine on subsequent sleep: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *Sleep Medicine Reviews*. 2023. PMID: 36870101. DOI: 10.1016/j.smrv.2023.101764\n3. Nehlig A. Interindividual differences in caffeine metabolism and factors driving caffeine consumption. *Pharmacological Reviews*. 2018. PMID: 29514871. DOI: 10.1124/pr.117.014407","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:3","member/quick","pillar/sommeil"]},{"slug":"light-sets-your-body-clock","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_light_sets_your_body_clock","title":"Light sets your body clock","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 4","summary":"Daylight first thing in the morning tells your body clock the day has started, and dimmer evenings let it wind down on time.","body":"Most of us think of sleep as something that happens at night. But a good night often starts in the morning. The light you see in the first minutes after waking helps decide how awake you feel during the day, and how sleepy you feel at bedtime. It costs nothing, takes five minutes, and you can do it in your pyjamas.\n\n## Your body runs on a clock\n\nInside your brain sits a small master clock. It keeps a rhythm of roughly 24 hours and sets the timing of many things: when you feel alert, when you feel sleepy, your body temperature, your hormones, even your appetite.\n\nThat clock is not perfectly accurate on its own. It needs a daily signal to stay in time with the outside world, and its strongest signal is light reaching your eyes. Light in the morning tells the clock \"the day has started\". Bright light late in the evening sends the opposite message, too late: it tells the clock the day is still going, and pushes your rhythm later.\n\nSo the pattern your body likes is simple: **bright days, dim evenings, dark nights**. An international group of light and sleep scientists put it in exactly those terms in their 2022 recommendations.\n\n## Why outside, and why first thing\n\nIndoor light is much weaker than daylight, even when a room feels bright to you. Outdoors, even on a grey Swiss morning, there is usually far more light than under a ceiling lamp. Window glass also takes some of it away. That is why the habit asks for fresh air: outside, on a balcony, or with the window open and your head out.\n\nTiming matters too. Light early in your day is the light that helps your clock run on time. If you start the day in a dim room and go straight to your phone, your clock gets a weak, confusing signal.\n\nThe fresh air and a few slow breaths are part of it. They are a calm way to arrive in the day before messages, news and to-do lists arrive.\n\n## The other half: screens at night\n\nWhat you do in the evening matters as much as the morning. Bright screens close to your eyes in the last hours before bed can delay your clock. In one study, people who read on a light-emitting tablet before bed took longer to fall asleep, made less melatonin (the hormone that signals night), and felt less alert the next morning than when they read a printed book.\n\nYou don't need to live in the dark. Lower the lights in the evening, keep screens further away, and give your last hour to something calmer. The 3-2-1 rule, also part of today, covers this.\n\n## What to do\n\nThe habit is **Daylight first thing: head out of the window, and breathe**.\n\n- Right when you wake up, before your phone.\n- Then 5 minutes outside, or at an open window.\n- No sunglasses; never look straight at the sun.\n\nYou only need to be in the daylight, looking around as you normally would. Never stare at the sun, at any time of day. If your eyes are sensitive to light or you have an eye condition, check with your eye doctor first. In winter, when it is still dark when you wake, step out as soon as it gets light, or pair the habit with your first walk of the day.\n\n## What to look for\n\nGive it a week or two. Many people notice:\n\n- They feel more awake in the morning, sooner.\n- It is easier to fall asleep at night.\n- Their mood feels steadier through the day.\n\nNote your energy and sleep in your evening check-in, and see what your Trends show after a few weeks.\n\n**Bright mornings and dim evenings are the simplest way to set your body clock: five minutes of daylight, before your phone, every day.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Brown TM, et al. Recommendations for daytime, evening, and nighttime indoor light exposure to best support physiology, sleep, and wakefulness in healthy adults. *PLoS Biology*. 2022. PMID: 35298459. DOI: 10.1371/journal.pbio.3001571\n2. Blume C, et al. Effects of light on human circadian rhythms, sleep and mood. *Somnologie*. 2019. PMID: 31534436. DOI: 10.1007/s11818-019-00215-x\n3. Chang AM, et al. Evening use of light-emitting eReaders negatively affects sleep, circadian timing, and next-morning alertness. *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the USA*. 2015. PMID: 25535358. DOI: 10.1073/pnas.1418490112","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/sommeil"]},{"slug":"the-3-2-1-rule","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_the_3_2_1_rule","title":"The 3-2-1 rule","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 4","summary":"Three simple time limits before bed, for food, hard exercise and screens, that give your body the quiet it needs to fall asleep.","body":"Falling asleep is not a switch. Your body slows down step by step through the evening: your temperature drops, your heart rate settles, your mind quietens. The 3-2-1 rule simply keeps three things out of the way of that slowdown. It is easy to remember, and you can count it back from your usual bedtime.\n\n## 3 hours: your last food\n\nDigestion is work. A big meal just before bed keeps your gut busy at the time your body wants to rest. Lying down with a full stomach also makes it easier for acid to come back up, which can mean heartburn and broken sleep. In one study from Japan, people with reflux were far more likely than others to go to bed within three hours of dinner.\n\nFinishing your last food about three hours before bed gives your stomach time to empty before you lie down.\n\n## 2 hours: no hard exercise\n\nExercise in the evening is not the enemy. Overall, studies show it does not harm sleep for most people. The exception is hard, intense effort finishing close to bedtime, which can leave you hot, wired and slower to drop off. Two hours is a comfortable margin to cool down.\n\nGentle movement is different. Stretching, light movement and a walk are fine, and often help. Your 2 minutes of gentle stretching before bed fits right in.\n\n## 1 hour: no scrolling\n\nScreens do two things at night. Their bright light close to your eyes can delay your body clock, and their content keeps your mind switched on: one more video, one more post, one more email. Using the phone in bed is linked with taking longer to fall asleep and feeling more tired the next day.\n\nThis is not about never touching your phone. Answering a message is fine. It's the stimulating stuff you leave: scrolling, news, games, work.\n\n## What to do\n\nThe habit is **The 3-2-1 rule: 3 h no food, 2 h no intense exercise, 1 h no screens**.\n\n- 3 hours before bed: your last food of the day.\n- 2 hours before: no hard exercise. Stretching, light movement and a walk are fine.\n- 1 hour before: no scrolling. Answering a message is fine; it's the stimulating stuff you leave.\n\nIf all three feel like a lot, start with the one that is easiest for you, then add the others.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Falling asleep faster.\n- Waking less in the night.\n- Calmer evenings.\n\n**Three limits, counted back from bedtime: give your body a quiet runway into sleep.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Fujiwara Y, et al. Association between dinner-to-bed time and gastro-esophageal reflux disease. *American Journal of Gastroenterology*. 2005. PMID: 16393212. DOI: 10.1111/j.1572-0241.2005.00354.x\n2. Stutz J, et al. Effects of evening exercise on sleep in healthy participants: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *Sports Medicine*. 2019. PMID: 30374942. DOI: 10.1007/s40279-018-1015-0\n3. Chang AM, et al. Evening use of light-emitting eReaders negatively affects sleep, circadian timing, and next-morning alertness. *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the USA*. 2015. PMID: 25535358. DOI: 10.1073/pnas.1418490112\n4. Exelmans L, et al. Bedtime mobile phone use and sleep in adults. *Social Science & Medicine*. 2016. PMID: 26688552. DOI: 10.1016/j.socscimed.2015.11.037","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:3","member/quick","pillar/sommeil"]},{"slug":"breathe-the-time-you-give-yourself","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_breathe_the_time_you_give_yourself","title":"Breathe: the time you give yourself","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 5","summary":"Five minutes of breathing a day is less about the perfect technique and more about giving yourself time, and training a calmer, more positive frame of mind.","body":"Most days are full from the moment you wake up. Messages, work, family, errands: there is always something asking for your attention. It is easy to go a whole day without one moment that is just for you. Today's habit gives you that moment: five minutes, once a day, with nothing to do but breathe.\n\n## Why your breath is a good place to start\n\nBreathing is one of the few things in your body that runs on its own and that you can also take over at will. You can't tell your heart to slow down or your stress to settle. But you can change how you breathe, and your body receives a different signal.\n\nYour state changes your breathing: when you are stressed, it tends to get fast and shallow. It works the other way too. Changing your breathing on purpose can change how you feel. That makes the breath a simple, free tool you carry everywhere.\n\n## The practice we offer\n\nTo start, we offer a round inspired by Wim Hof, with a guided video, *Guided breathing, with Wim Hof*. A round looks like this: a series of deep, full breaths, then a slow breath out, then a pause, holding only as long as is comfortable. Breathe normally for a moment between rounds. Two rounds are enough to start.\n\nMany people like it because it is lively. You will often feel tingling, warmth or a light head during the deep breaths. That is common with fast, deep breathing, and it passes when you breathe normally again.\n\nTo be honest about the evidence: research on Wim Hof-style breathing is still limited. A 2024 systematic review found only eight small trials. Some suggest effects on the body's inflammation response, but several of those studies combined the breathing with cold exposure and meditation, and results on other measures were mixed. Wider research on breathwork in general is more encouraging: a review of randomised trials found it was linked with lower stress, anxiety and low mood, while its authors warned against the hype.\n\n## It's not about the technique\n\nHere is the most important part. The exact method matters less than you might think. In a one-month study, people who did five minutes of structured breathing every day reported a better mood. Three different breathing styles were tested, and a calm, exhale-focused one did especially well. What they had in common was a short, regular moment of attention to the breath.\n\nSo if the Wim Hof round doesn't suit you, slow breathing with a long breath out is just as good a choice. Later in the track, in your evening routine, you will meet box breathing too.\n\nWhat really counts is the time you give yourself. Five minutes that say: \"I matter enough to stop.\"\n\n## A more positive attitude, one moment at a time\n\nA positive attitude is not about pretending everything is fine. It's about where your attention goes, and attention can be trained, a little like a muscle.\n\nA few ways to use your five minutes for that:\n\n- **Arrive.** Before you start, notice how you feel, without judging it. Tired, tense, flat: all are fine.\n- **Breathe.** Do your rounds. Let the busy thoughts come and go.\n- **Close with one kind thought.** One thing you are looking forward to, or one thing that went well. Then go back to your day.\n\nDone once, it is a pleasant pause. Done daily, many people find their default mood shifts: a little more patience, a little less rush, a quicker return to calm after a hard moment. Tonight's habit, *Write down three good things about your day*, works the same way from the other end of the day.\n\n## Safety first\n\nSit or lie down. Never practise in water, while driving or while standing. If you are pregnant, or have heart problems or epilepsy, skip this practice unless your doctor agrees. If you feel dizzy or unwell, stop and breathe normally.\n\n## What to do\n\nThe habit is **5 minutes of breathing, once a day**.\n\n- Sit or lie down; never in water or while driving.\n- 30 deep breaths, then breathe out slowly.\n- Hold as long as is comfortable; 2 rounds to start.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- A calmer, clearer head.\n- Energy without coffee.\n- A moment that is yours.\n\n**The technique is just the door: the real practice is giving yourself five minutes, every day.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Almahayni O, et al. Does the Wim Hof Method have a beneficial impact on physiological and psychological outcomes in healthy and non-healthy participants? A systematic review. *PLoS One*. 2024. PMID: 38478473. DOI: 10.1371/journal.pone.0286933\n2. Fincham GW, et al. Effect of breathwork on stress and mental health: a meta-analysis of randomised-controlled trials. *Scientific Reports*. 2023. PMID: 36624160. DOI: 10.1038/s41598-022-27247-y\n3. Balban MY, et al. Brief structured respiration practices enhance mood and reduce physiological arousal. *Cell Reports Medicine*. 2023. PMID: 36630953. DOI: 10.1016/j.xcrm.2022.100895","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:5","pillar/stress"]},{"slug":"three-good-things","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_three_good_things","title":"Three good things","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 5","summary":"Writing down three good things about your day each evening gently trains your attention towards what goes well.","body":"At the end of a day, the mind tends to replay what went wrong: the tense meeting, the thing you forgot, the to-do list for tomorrow. That's normal. Our brains are built to spot problems. But if that is the last thing you think about before sleep, it colours your evening and often your night. This small habit gives your mind something else to hold on to.\n\n## Why it works\n\nWhat you pay attention to shapes how you feel. Most good moments in a day are small and pass quickly: a kind word, a good coffee, sun on your face, a task finished. If you don't stop to notice them, they are gone by the evening.\n\nWriting them down does three things. It makes you look for them. It makes you remember them. And over time, it trains you to spot them as they happen, not only at night.\n\n## What the research shows\n\n\"Three good things\" is one of the best-known exercises in positive psychology. In a large online trial, people wrote down three things that went well each day, and why, for one week. It was one of the exercises that raised happiness and eased symptoms of low mood, and the effects lasted for months.\n\nLater reviews are more modest. A meta-analysis of gratitude exercises found they improve wellbeing a little, on average. That is an honest picture: it is a small daily tool, not a cure. But small and daily is exactly what this track is built on. There is also a link with sleep: people who feel more grateful tend to have calmer, more positive thoughts as they fall asleep, and sleep better.\n\n## What to do\n\nThe habit is **Write down three good things about your day**.\n\n- In the evening, in your phone or a journal.\n- Small things count.\n- One line on why it was good.\n\nA paper journal by the bed works well, and it keeps you off the screen in your last hour. If you use your phone, write your three lines, then put it away.\n\nSome evenings it will feel hard to find three. That's fine. \"I had a quiet lunch\" counts. Later in the track, *The habit of writing* builds on this with a few more minutes for what's on your mind.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Falling asleep on a good thought.\n- Over weeks, a more positive outlook.\n- Noticing what helps you.\n\n**Three small lines each evening teach your mind to notice what goes well.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Seligman MEP, et al. Positive psychology progress: empirical validation of interventions. *American Psychologist*. 2005. PMID: 16045394. DOI: 10.1037/0003-066X.60.5.410\n2. Davis DE, et al. Thankful for the little things: a meta-analysis of gratitude interventions. *Journal of Counseling Psychology*. 2016. PMID: 26575348. DOI: 10.1037/cou0000107\n3. Wood AM, et al. Gratitude influences sleep through the mechanism of pre-sleep cognitions. *Journal of Psychosomatic Research*. 2009. PMID: 19073292. DOI: 10.1016/j.jpsychores.2008.09.002","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:3","member/quick","pillar/stress"]},{"slug":"simple-actions-repeated","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_simple_actions_repeated","title":"Simple actions, repeated","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 6","summary":"The basics, done most days, do more for you than a perfect plan you can't keep.","body":"You have reached the end of your first week. There is nothing new to add today. Today is for looking at what you have already done, and for one idea that sits under the whole track: simple actions, repeated, are what change how you feel.\n\n## The trouble with the perfect plan\n\nMost of us have started a perfect plan at some point. A new diet, a training programme, a morning routine with ten steps. It works well for a week or two. Then life happens: a late meeting, a sick child, a trip. The plan needs everything to go right, so it breaks the first time something goes wrong.\n\nA plan like that also asks you to decide, every day, whether you have the time and the energy. Every one of those decisions is a chance to say \"not today\".\n\nThe habits in this track are built the other way round. They are small enough to fit into a bad day. Three minutes. A glass of water. Your head out of the window. That is not because small is all you can do. It is because small is what you can do every day.\n\n## Your body reads signals, every day\n\nYour body adapts to what you do often, not to what you do once. Morning light, the timing of your meals, a short walk, a slow breath, a regular bedtime: each one is a signal. One signal on its own does little. The same signal, day after day, is something your body can organise around.\n\nThis is the idea of the minimum effective dose: the smallest action that still sends a clear signal. A 5-minute walk after eating. Two minutes of stretching before bed. No caffeine after 16:00. None of these looks impressive. Repeated, they add up to more regular days, and regular days are where better energy, sleep and digestion tend to come from.\n\n## Repetition makes it easier\n\nThere is a second reason to repeat the same simple things. The more often you do something in the same situation, the less you have to think about it. Research on habits describes this well: behaviour repeated in a stable context gradually becomes automatic, and automatic behaviour no longer depends on willpower or motivation on the day.\n\nThat is the quiet goal of these two weeks. Not to do more, but to make a few good things easy enough that they happen on their own. Tomorrow, in **Your day, on one page**, you will see how this week's habits fit together into one ordinary day.\n\n## What to do\n\nNo new habit today. Use the time to look back at your week:\n\n- Look at which habits happened on most days. Those are already becoming yours.\n- Pick the one that felt easiest. Keep it exactly as it is.\n- Pick the one that felt hardest. Make it smaller rather than dropping it: one minute instead of three, one good thing instead of three.\n- If you missed some days, start again today. Nothing is lost.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice, by the end of the first week:\n\n- A habit or two that now happens without a reminder.\n- That some days feel more regular: waking, eating and going to bed at more similar times.\n- That small things are easier to restart after a missed day than a big plan.\n\nIf you have a health condition and are unsure whether a habit suits you, ask your doctor or nutritionist.\n\n**Simple actions, done most days, beat the perfect plan.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Wood W, Rünger D. Psychology of Habit. *Annual Review of Psychology*. 2016;67:289–314. PMID: 26361052. DOI: 10.1146/annurev-psych-122414-033417\n2. Gardner B, Lally P, Wardle J. Making health habitual: the psychology of 'habit-formation' and general practice. *British Journal of General Practice*. 2012;62(605):664–666. PMID: 23211256. DOI: 10.3399/bjgp12X659466\n3. Hill JO. Can a small-changes approach help address the obesity epidemic? A report of the Joint Task Force of the American Society for Nutrition, Institute of Food Technologists, and International Food Information Council. *American Journal of Clinical Nutrition*. 2009;89(2):477–484. PMID: 19088151. DOI: 10.3945/ajcn.2008.26566","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4"]},{"slug":"the-power-of-compounding","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_the_power_of_compounding","title":"The power of compounding","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 6","summary":"Small gains, repeated, add up over weeks, and the first two weeks are when the groundwork is laid.","body":"At the end of a first week, it is normal to wonder whether any of this is doing much. A few minutes of light, a short walk, some slow breaths. Each one feels small, and on any single day it is. The point is not the single day. It is what the days add up to.\n\n## Small, then smaller, then a lot\n\nCompounding is the idea that small gains build on each other. Money in a savings account is the classic example: the interest earns interest, so growth is slow at first and faster later.\n\nYou may have seen the claim that getting \"1% better every day\" makes you 37 times better after a year. That figure is arithmetic, not biology. It is an illustration, not a promise: bodies don't improve in neat percentages, and nobody gets 37 times better at sleeping. But the idea underneath it holds. Small changes kept up for a long time can matter more than big changes kept up for a short time. Experts on weight and lifestyle have argued for exactly this \"small changes\" approach, because small changes are the ones people manage to keep.\n\n## How your habits build on each other\n\nIn your body, compounding looks less like a curve and more like a chain:\n\n- Daylight in the morning and no caffeine after 16:00 can make it easier to fall asleep.\n- Better sleep tends to mean more energy and fewer cravings the next day.\n- More energy makes the morning movement and the walk after eating easier to do.\n- Moving more during the day can help you feel ready for sleep at night.\n\nNone of these links is dramatic. Together, they can move a whole day in the right direction, and then the next one.\n\n## Why the first two weeks matter more than they feel\n\nTwo weeks is not long enough to see the full effect of a habit, and it is not long enough for most habits to feel automatic. Research suggests that often takes around two months, with big differences from person to person.\n\nSo why do the first two weeks matter? Because the early repetitions are where most of the work happens. When people track how automatic a new habit feels, it tends to climb fastest in the early weeks and then level off. In a study of new gym members, an exercise habit took hold in those who went at least four times a week for about six weeks. Consistency early on was what counted.\n\nIn other words, the first two weeks don't look like much from the outside. Underneath, they are when the habit takes root.\n\n## What to do\n\nNo new habit today. Think of your habits as small deposits:\n\n- Look at which habits you did on most days this week. Those are your regular deposits.\n- Keep them going into week two, even on busy days. A smaller version still counts.\n- Don't judge them by one day. Look at the whole week.\n- If you miss a day, make the next deposit. The account is still open.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice, over the first weeks:\n\n- Days that feel a little more even: steadier energy, an easier evening.\n- One habit making another easier, such as a better night making the morning flow more appealing.\n- That the habits take less effort to start than they did on day one.\n\n**Small gains, kept up, add up: the first two weeks lay the foundation for everything after.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Hill JO. Can a small-changes approach help address the obesity epidemic? A report of the Joint Task Force of the American Society for Nutrition, Institute of Food Technologists, and International Food Information Council. *American Journal of Clinical Nutrition*. 2009;89(2):477–484. PMID: 19088151. DOI: 10.3945/ajcn.2008.26566\n2. Singh B, Murphy A, Maher C, Smith AE. Time to Form a Habit: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Health Behaviour Habit Formation and Its Determinants. *Healthcare (Basel)*. 2024;12(23):2488. PMID: 39685110. DOI: 10.3390/healthcare12232488\n3. Kaushal N, Rhodes RE. Exercise habit formation in new gym members: a longitudinal study. *Journal of Behavioral Medicine*. 2015;38(4):652–663. PMID: 25851609. DOI: 10.1007/s10865-015-9640-7\n4. Gardner B, Lally P, Wardle J. Making health habitual: the psychology of 'habit-formation' and general practice. *British Journal of General Practice*. 2012;62(605):664–666. PMID: 23211256. DOI: 10.3399/bjgp12X659466","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4"]},{"slug":"the-power-of-habits","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_the_power_of_habits","title":"The power of habits","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 6","summary":"A habit is a small action tied to a cue you already have, repeated until it runs on its own.","body":"Think of brushing your teeth. You don't decide to do it each night, weigh the pros and cons, or need to feel motivated. It just happens, because it always happens at that moment. That is what a habit is, and it is why habits are so useful for health: they keep working on the days when motivation doesn't.\n\n## Cue, routine, reward\n\nHabits are often described in three parts:\n\n- **The cue**: the moment that sets it off. Waking up, sitting down to eat, switching off the light.\n- **The routine**: the action itself. Daylight at the window, three breaths before the meal, a slow breath out in bed.\n- **The reward**: what makes it worth repeating. Feeling more awake, a lighter stomach, a calmer head.\n\nHabit research shows that the cue matters most. When you repeat the same action in the same situation, your brain starts to link the two. Over time, the situation alone is enough to start the action, without a decision. The reward helps at the start, while you are still choosing to do it. Later, the link carries it.\n\n## Anchor the new to the old\n\nThe easiest cue is one you already have. You wake up every day. You eat every day. You go to bed every day. These are strong, reliable moments, and the track ties each habit to one of them:\n\n- After you wake up: **Daylight first thing: head out of the window, and breathe**.\n- When you sit down to eat: **Mindful eating: no phone, chew, breathe, a glass of water**.\n- After the meal: **A 5-minute walk after eating**.\n- In bed, lights off: **Optional: a mini breath before sleep**.\n\nA useful way to set this up is a simple sentence: \"After I [something I already do], I will [the new habit].\" The clearer the moment, the less there is to remember.\n\n## Why we start small\n\nA small habit is easy to do on a bad day, and doing it on a bad day is what makes it stick. Studies of habit formation find that how consistently you repeat something, and how simple it is, both predict how strong the habit becomes. That is why the track starts with three minutes and not thirty. You can always do more once the habit is in place. It is much harder to keep something that felt like a chore from the first day.\n\n## Missing a day doesn't break it\n\nHabits take time. In one well-known study, people who repeated a simple daily health behaviour of their choice, at the same moment each day, felt it become automatic after an average of about 66 days, with large differences between people. A recent review found a similar picture: often around two months, sometimes much less, sometimes much more.\n\nThe same study found something reassuring: missing the occasional day did not seriously set people back. The habit kept building once they started again. So if you missed a day this week, don't start over in your head. Just pick it up at the next cue.\n\n## What to do\n\nNo new habit today. Look at this week's habits and check each one has a clear cue:\n\n- For each habit, name the moment it belongs to: waking, a meal, the afternoon, bedtime.\n- If a habit keeps slipping, tie it to a moment you never miss.\n- If it still feels like a chore, make it smaller.\n- If you missed a day, do it at the next cue. No catching up needed.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice, as the days go on:\n\n- A habit that starts before you have thought about it.\n- That the cue begins to \"remind\" you on its own.\n- That restarting after a missed day gets easier.\n\n**Tie it to a moment you already have, keep it small, and pick it up again after a missed day.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Gardner B, Lally P, Wardle J. Making health habitual: the psychology of 'habit-formation' and general practice. *British Journal of General Practice*. 2012;62(605):664–666. PMID: 23211256. DOI: 10.3399/bjgp12X659466\n2. Singh B, Murphy A, Maher C, Smith AE. Time to Form a Habit: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis of Health Behaviour Habit Formation and Its Determinants. *Healthcare (Basel)*. 2024;12(23):2488. PMID: 39685110. DOI: 10.3390/healthcare12232488\n3. Kaushal N, Rhodes RE. Exercise habit formation in new gym members: a longitudinal study. *Journal of Behavioral Medicine*. 2015;38(4):652–663. PMID: 25851609. DOI: 10.1007/s10865-015-9640-7\n4. Wood W, Rünger D. Psychology of Habit. *Annual Review of Psychology*. 2016;67:289–314. PMID: 26361052. DOI: 10.1146/annurev-psych-122414-033417","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4"]},{"slug":"your-day-on-one-page","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_your_day_on_one_page","title":"Your day, on one page","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 7","summary":"Your whole day in four moments, morning to evening, and how each habit makes the next one easier.","body":"Over the first week you added habits one pillar at a time: nutrition, movement, sleep, then mind. Seen one by one, they can feel like a long list. Seen as a day, they are something simpler: a few small moments, each tied to something you already do. Today you also make two upgrades: the 5-minute morning flow, and an exercise snack every day.\n\n## Morning: wake the body, set the clock\n\n- **Daylight first thing: head out of the window, and breathe.** Right when you wake up, before your phone.\n- **5-minute morning flow.** New today. Five minutes inspired by qi gong: breathe with the arms, twist, shake it out. It takes over from your 3 minutes of movement.\n\nMorning light helps set your body clock for the day, which is part of what makes you sleepy at the right time tonight. The flow loosens the body after the night and gives you a first win before the day gets busy.\n\n## Midday (10:00–14:00): eat calmly, then move\n\n- **Mindful eating: no phone, chew, breathe, a glass of water.**\n- **A 5-minute walk after eating.** Right after the meal, or within half an hour.\n- **5 minutes of breathing, once a day.** Sitting or lying down.\n- **One exercise snack a day.** New today, and no longer optional: 10 squats, a minute of stairs, or 10 wall push-ups.\n\nA calm meal helps digestion. A short walk afterwards helps your muscles use the energy from the meal, which can soften the energy dip that often follows. In one study of people with type 2 diabetes, a 10-minute walk after each meal lowered the rise in blood sugar after eating more than one 30-minute walk at another time of day. The exercise snack adds a short burst of effort. In a large study of people who didn't exercise, those who did a few vigorous bursts of 1 to 2 minutes in their everyday life had a lower risk of dying early. That kind of study shows a link, not proof, but it fits with what we see: a little effort, often, is worth having.\n\n## Afternoon: protect tonight's sleep\n\n- **No caffeine after 16:00.** Decaf, herbal tea or water after that.\n\nCaffeine stays in the body for hours. Stopping by 16:00 gives it time to wear off before bed. If you are tempted to reach for a coffee in the afternoon, notice whether the midday walk and a steadier lunch make that dip smaller.\n\n## Evening: slow down\n\n- **The 3-2-1 rule: 3 h no food, 2 h no intense exercise, 1 h no screens.**\n- **2 minutes of gentle stretching before bed.**\n- **Write down three good things about your day.**\n- **Optional: a mini breath before sleep.** In bed, lights off.\n\nTogether, these tell your body the day is over: digestion has finished, the heart rate has settled, the mind has a good thought to end on.\n\n## How the habits link up\n\nThe day works as a loop. Morning light and no late caffeine help you fall asleep. Better sleep tends to mean more energy and fewer cravings tomorrow. More energy makes the morning flow and the exercise snack easier. Moving after meals helps keep energy steadier through the afternoon, so you need less caffeine. A calmer midday makes for a calmer evening.\n\nEach habit is also tied to a moment you already have: waking up, a meal, bedtime. You don't need to remember a list. You need to notice the moment.\n\n## What to do\n\n**5-minute morning flow**\n- Slow breathing with the arms\n- Gentle twists, left and right\n- Shake it out\n\n**One exercise snack a day**\n- 10 squats, a minute of stairs, or 10 wall push-ups\n- Once a day, whenever suits you\n- Easier is always fine\n\nMove gently and stop if anything hurts. If you have a heart, joint or balance problem, ask your doctor before adding more effort.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- A loose, awake body and a better mood in the morning.\n- Clarity of mind and more energy after an exercise snack.\n- That one good moment makes the next one easier.\n\n**Four moments, each tied to something you already do: that is your whole day.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Reynolds AN, Mann JI, Williams S, Venn BJ. Advice to walk after meals is more effective for lowering postprandial glycaemia in type 2 diabetes mellitus than advice that does not specify timing: a randomised crossover study. *Diabetologia*. 2016;59(12):2572–2578. PMID: 27747394. DOI: 10.1007/s00125-016-4085-2\n2. Stamatakis E, et al. Association of wearable device-measured vigorous intermittent lifestyle physical activity with mortality. *Nature Medicine*. 2022;28(12):2521–2529. PMID: 36482104. DOI: 10.1038/s41591-022-02100-x\n3. Gardner B, Lally P, Wardle J. Making health habitual: the psychology of 'habit-formation' and general practice. *British Journal of General Practice*. 2012;62(605):664–666. PMID: 23211256. DOI: 10.3399/bjgp12X659466","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:5"]},{"slug":"complex-or-refined-carbs-why-it-matters","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_complex_or_refined_carbs_why_it_matters","title":"Complex or refined carbs: why it matters","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 8","summary":"Choosing whole, fibre-rich carbohydrates over refined ones gives you a steadier energy curve and can soften the afternoon dip.","body":"Carbohydrates are not the enemy. They are your body's main fuel, especially for your brain and your muscles when you move. The question is not whether to eat them, but which kind. Two lunches with the same amount of carbohydrate can leave you feeling very different by three o'clock.\n\n## Complex and refined: what's the difference?\n\n**Complex carbohydrates** come in foods that are close to how they grew: oats, wholegrain bread, brown rice, quinoa, beans, lentils, whole fruit. The starch or sugar is packed in with fibre, and often with protein, vitamins and minerals.\n\n**Refined carbohydrates** have had most of that removed. White bread, white pasta, many breakfast cereals, pastries, sweets and fruit juice are examples. The grain has lost its outer layers, or the fruit has lost its pulp, and much of the fibre has gone with them.\n\nThe difference is not the carbohydrate itself. It is everything that comes with it.\n\n## Fibre slows things down\n\nFibre is the part of plant food your body can't fully digest. It slows down how quickly the carbohydrate is broken down and absorbed. That means a gentler rise in blood sugar after the meal, rather than a quick peak and a faster drop.\n\nFibre also feeds the bacteria in your gut and adds bulk, which helps keep digestion regular. And it matters over the long term. A large series of reviews, covering more than 180 long-term studies, found that people who ate the most fibre had lower rates of heart disease, stroke, type 2 diabetes and bowel cancer than those who ate the least. Those studies show a link rather than proof, but the picture is consistent, and wholegrain foods show a similar pattern.\n\n## The energy curve and the afternoon dip\n\nMany people know the feeling: a heavy lunch, then a slump in the middle of the afternoon, and a reach for coffee or something sweet.\n\nThe afternoon dip has more than one cause. Your body clock has a natural lull in the early afternoon, and a short night makes it worse. But lunch can make it deeper or softer. A meal built on refined carbohydrates tends to give a faster rise and fall in blood sugar. A meal built on complex carbohydrates, with protein and vegetables, tends to give a flatter, longer curve. Many people find their afternoon steadier that way. A short walk after the meal helps too, as you will see in **The walk after every meal**.\n\n## Simple swaps\n\nYou don't need to give anything up. Swap one thing at a time:\n\n- White bread → wholegrain or rye bread.\n- White pasta and rice → wholegrain pasta, brown rice or quinoa.\n- Sugary cereal → oats, porridge or muesli without added sugar.\n- Fruit juice → a piece of whole fruit.\n- Add beans, lentils or chickpeas to soups, salads and sauces.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Oats, wholegrain bread, beans, quinoa, brown rice\n- White bread and pasta → wholegrain\n- Whole fruit rather than juice\n\nIf you are not used to much fibre, increase it over a week or two and drink enough water, to avoid bloating. If you have a gut condition, or take medication for diabetes, ask your doctor or nutritionist before making big changes.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Steadier energy through the day.\n- No three o'clock dip, or a smaller one.\n- Better digestion, with the fibre.\n\n**Keep the carbohydrates, change the kind: whole and fibre-rich over refined.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Reynolds A, Mann J, Cummings J, Winter N, Mete E, Te Morenga L. Carbohydrate quality and human health: a series of systematic reviews and meta-analyses. *Lancet*. 2019;393(10170):434–445. PMID: 30638909. DOI: 10.1016/S0140-6736(18)31809-9\n2. Aune D, et al. Whole grain consumption and risk of cardiovascular disease, cancer, and all cause and cause specific mortality: systematic review and dose-response meta-analysis of prospective studies. *BMJ*. 2016;353:i2716. PMID: 27301975. DOI: 10.1136/bmj.i2716\n3. Augustin LSA, et al. Glycemic index, glycemic load and glycemic response: an International Scientific Consensus Summit from the International Carbohydrate Quality Consortium (ICQC). *Nutrition, Metabolism and Cardiovascular Diseases*. 2015;25(9):795–815. PMID: 26160327. DOI: 10.1016/j.numecd.2015.05.005","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/nutrition"]},{"slug":"protein-at-every-meal-and-why-it-matters","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_protein_at_every_meal_and_why_it_matters","title":"Protein at every meal, and why it matters","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 8","summary":"A palm-sized portion of protein at each meal gives your body building material, keeps you fuller and helps protect your muscle as you age.","body":"Week two is about building your plate. We start with protein, because it is the part of the meal that most often goes missing. Many people eat most of their protein at dinner, while breakfast is toast, cereal or a coffee on the way out. Spreading it across the day is one of the simplest changes you can make.\n\n## Your body's building material\n\nWhen you eat protein, your body breaks it down into amino acids. These are used to repair and build muscle, but also to make enzymes, connective tissue, immune proteins and much more. Your body doesn't store protein the way it stores energy, so it relies on a regular supply.\n\nA simple way to think about it: food gives you fuel and building blocks. Carbohydrates and fats are mostly fuel. Protein is mostly building blocks. Training and daily movement send the signal to repair and strengthen. Protein supplies the material for the response.\n\n## Fuller for longer\n\nProtein tends to be more filling than the same amount of energy from carbohydrate or fat. Reviews of feeding studies find a modest effect: people feel fuller and their fullness hormones rise after a higher-protein meal. It is not magic, and it won't override everything else you eat. But a breakfast with eggs or yogurt often carries people through to lunch better than one made only of bread or cereal, and that can mean fewer cravings mid-morning.\n\n## Muscle, as we age\n\nFrom middle age onwards, muscle mass and strength tend to decline slowly, and in later life that loss can affect balance, energy and independence. Movement is the main signal to keep muscle. Enough protein is what lets your body act on that signal. Expert groups on ageing advise that older adults generally need more protein than younger adults, not less.\n\nHow you spread it may matter too. In one small study, adults who ate a similar amount of protein at breakfast, lunch and dinner built more muscle protein over the day than when most of it came at dinner. It was a small study, so we take it as a pointer rather than a rule, but it supports the simple idea of some protein at every meal.\n\n## Easy sources, meal by meal\n\nYou don't need to weigh anything. Use your hand: a portion about the size and thickness of your palm.\n\n- **Breakfast**: eggs, Greek yogurt, quark or cottage cheese, smoked salmon, a tofu scramble.\n- **Lunch**: chicken, tinned fish such as sardines or tuna, lentils, chickpeas or beans, tofu, a couple of eggs on a salad.\n- **Dinner**: fish, chicken, beans or lentils, tofu, eggs.\n\nPlant sources count. Beans and lentils also bring fibre, which you will read about in **Complex or refined carbs: why it matters**. If you eat mostly plants, mixing sources across the day helps.\n\n## What to do\n\n- Eggs, yogurt, fish, chicken, tofu, beans or lentils\n- About the size of your palm\n- Start with breakfast\n\nIf you have kidney disease, or have been told to watch your protein, ask your doctor or nutritionist before changing how much you eat.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Feeling fuller for longer after meals.\n- Fewer cravings, especially mid-morning and late afternoon.\n- Over the long run, the benefit you can't feel day to day: keeping your muscle as you age.\n\n**A palm of protein at every meal, starting with breakfast.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Leidy HJ, et al. The role of protein in weight loss and maintenance. *American Journal of Clinical Nutrition*. 2015;101(6):1320S–1329S. PMID: 25926512. DOI: 10.3945/ajcn.114.084038\n2. Mamerow MM, et al. Dietary protein distribution positively influences 24-h muscle protein synthesis in healthy adults. *Journal of Nutrition*. 2014;144(6):876–880. PMID: 24477298. DOI: 10.3945/jn.113.185280\n3. Bauer J, et al. Evidence-based recommendations for optimal dietary protein intake in older people: a position paper from the PROT-AGE Study Group. *Journal of the American Medical Directors Association*. 2013;14(8):542–559. PMID: 23867520. DOI: 10.1016/j.jamda.2013.05.021\n4. Cruz-Jentoft AJ, et al. Sarcopenia: revised European consensus on definition and diagnosis. *Age and Ageing*. 2019;48(1):16–31. PMID: 30312372. DOI: 10.1093/ageing/afy169","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/nutrition"]},{"slug":"the-walk-after-every-meal","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_the_walk_after_every_meal","title":"The walk after every meal","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 8","summary":"You started with one 5-minute walk after eating; now it becomes a short walk after every meal.","body":"On day 2 you added **A 5-minute walk after eating**. For many people it is one of the habits they notice first: a lighter stomach, less of the post-lunch slump. In week two, the same idea grows a little. Not a longer walk, but more of them: a few minutes after breakfast, lunch and dinner.\n\n## Why after the meal\n\nAfter you eat, your body breaks the carbohydrate in your meal down into glucose, which enters your blood. Working muscles take up glucose from the blood. So when you walk after a meal, your legs help use some of that energy, and the rise in blood sugar tends to be gentler.\n\nYou don't need to walk far or fast for this. A review of one-day studies found that breaking up sitting with short bouts of light walking lowered the rise in blood sugar and insulin after meals, compared with sitting still. The evidence is strongest for blood sugar. For digestion and comfort, your own experience is the best guide.\n\n## Why every meal\n\nTiming seems to matter. In one study of adults with type 2 diabetes, people were asked either to walk 30 minutes once a day, or 10 minutes after each main meal. The total was the same. The walks after meals lowered blood sugar after eating more, and the difference was largest after the evening meal, when people ate the most carbohydrate and tended to sit the longest.\n\nThat study was in people with diabetes, so we don't claim the same numbers for everyone. But the principle is simple and safe: move a little after each time you eat.\n\n## Making it fit\n\n- After breakfast: a few minutes on the way to work, or around the house.\n- After lunch: around the block, or the long way back to your desk.\n- After dinner: a gentle stroll. It fits with **The 3-2-1 rule**, since a walk is fine before bed.\n\n## What to do\n\n- 5 to 10 minutes\n- Right after eating, or within half an hour\n- Even around the house\n\nKeep the pace easy. If you feel discomfort walking straight after a big meal, wait a little longer or slow down.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Steadier energy all day.\n- Easier digestion.\n- More steps without trying.\n\n**A few easy minutes on your feet, after every meal.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Reynolds AN, Mann JI, Williams S, Venn BJ. Advice to walk after meals is more effective for lowering postprandial glycaemia in type 2 diabetes mellitus than advice that does not specify timing: a randomised crossover study. *Diabetologia*. 2016;59(12):2572–2578. PMID: 27747394. DOI: 10.1007/s00125-016-4085-2\n2. Buffey AJ, Herring MP, Langley CK, Donnelly AE, Carson BP. The Acute Effects of Interrupting Prolonged Sitting Time in Adults with Standing and Light-Intensity Walking on Biomarkers of Cardiometabolic Health in Adults: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis. *Sports Medicine*. 2022;52(8):1765–1787. PMID: 35147898. DOI: 10.1007/s40279-022-01649-4","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:3","member/quick","pillar/nutrition"]},{"slug":"muscle-a-long-term-investment","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_muscle_a_long_term_investment","title":"Muscle, a long-term investment","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 9","summary":"Your muscle is a health account you pay into for life, and small, regular movement moments are the deposits that protect your joints, your blood sugar and your independence.","body":"*Your muscle is a health account you pay into for life, and small, regular deposits count.*\n\nMost people think of muscle as something for the gym, or for how you look. In daily life it is much more ordinary than that. It carries the shopping up the stairs. It gets you out of a low chair. It catches you when you trip on the pavement. It is also one of the busiest tissues for your metabolism, working quietly after every meal.\n\nA helpful way to see it: muscle is an account. What you pay in now is what you draw on later.\n\n## Why think of muscle as an account\n\nMuscle responds to use. When you use it, your body keeps it, and can even build more. When you leave it unused, your body slowly lets it go. This happens so gradually that most people only notice when something that used to be easy, like the stairs or getting up from the floor, starts to feel hard.\n\nDoctors have a name for serious loss of muscle strength and mass: sarcopenia. The European consensus on it describes changes that build up across a whole lifetime, not only in old age, and puts low *strength* at the centre, ahead of size. So the aim is not big muscles. It is muscles that work.\n\nThe good news is that the account never closes. A position statement from the National Strength and Conditioning Association concludes that strength training helps people keep strength, mobility and independence well into older age. It is never too late to start paying in.\n\n## What your account pays for\n\n**Your joints.** Muscles around a joint share the load and steady the movement. Strong thighs and hips support the knees; a strong back and middle support the spine. Many people find their joints feel more comfortable as the muscles around them get stronger.\n\n**Your blood sugar.** Muscle is one of the main places your body sends sugar from a meal. Working muscle draws sugar out of the blood. In a large US survey of more than 13,000 adults, people with more muscle relative to their body size had better insulin sensitivity (how well the body handles sugar) and less prediabetes. That is a link, not proof of cause, but it fits what we know about how muscle works.\n\n**Your independence.** Getting up from the floor, carrying a child or a suitcase, keeping your balance on uneven ground: all of these draw on strength. In a review of large population studies, adults who did muscle-strengthening activities had a 10 to 17% lower risk of early death, heart disease, diabetes and cancer. Again, these studies show a link, and people who do strength work may differ in other ways too. But the direction is consistent.\n\n## Movement moments are the deposits\n\nYou do not need a gym to pay in. Every squat, every calf raise, every push-up against the wall is a small deposit. On their own they look modest. Repeated, day after day, they add up.\n\nEarly on, much of what changes is not the muscle itself but your nervous system learning to use it. As the library article *Strength Is More Than Muscle Size* explains, strength is what your muscles and nervous system produce together, and practice improves coordination and control. That is why simple moves feel easier within a couple of weeks.\n\nTwo small tips make each deposit count. Lower yourself slowly: the lowering part of a squat or calf raise is real work, not a rest. And give your body the building material, with protein at every meal (see *Protein at every meal, and why it matters*).\n\n## What to do\n\nYour habit from today: **2 to 3 movement moments a day**. It can be as light as a walk.\n\n- Choose a movement: it can be as light as a walk.\n- Or a few easy moves: squats, calf raises, a chest opener.\n- Push-ups too, if you're comfortable.\n\nStop if anything hurts. If you have a joint problem, a heart condition or are recovering from an injury, ask your doctor or your nutritionist first.\n\n## What to look for\n\nOver the next days and weeks, many people notice:\n\n- Clarity of mind after a movement moment.\n- Stable blood sugar, which you may feel as fewer dips after meals.\n- More energy through the day.\n- A stronger body overall: stairs, bags and getting up from a chair start to feel easier.\n\n**Every movement moment is a deposit in the account you will draw on for the rest of your life.**\n\n## References\n\n- Sarcopenia: revised European consensus on definition and diagnosis. Cruz-Jentoft AJ et al. *Age and Ageing*, 2019. PMID: 30312372. DOI: [10.1093/ageing/afy169](https://doi.org/10.1093/ageing/afy169)\n- Resistance Training for Older Adults: Position Statement From the National Strength and Conditioning Association. Fragala MS et al. *Journal of Strength and Conditioning Research*, 2019. PMID: 31343601. DOI: [10.1519/JSC.0000000000003230](https://doi.org/10.1519/JSC.0000000000003230)\n- Relative muscle mass is inversely associated with insulin resistance and prediabetes. Findings from the third National Health and Nutrition Examination Survey. Srikanthan P et al. *Journal of Clinical Endocrinology and Metabolism*, 2011. PMID: 21778224. DOI: [10.1210/jc.2011-0435](https://doi.org/10.1210/jc.2011-0435)\n- Muscle-strengthening activities are associated with lower risk and mortality in major non-communicable diseases: a systematic review and meta-analysis of cohort studies. Momma H et al. *British Journal of Sports Medicine*, 2022. PMID: 35228201. DOI: [10.1136/bjsports-2021-105061](https://doi.org/10.1136/bjsports-2021-105061)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/mouvement"]},{"slug":"the-benefits-of-exercise-snacks","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_the_benefits_of_exercise_snacks","title":"The benefits of exercise snacks","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 9","summary":"Short bursts of movement, two or three times a day, support your heart, steady your blood sugar and lift your energy, focus and mood, without needing a full workout.","body":"*A few short bursts of movement each day do more for your heart, blood sugar, energy and mood than their size suggests.*\n\nOn day 3 you met the idea in *Exercise snacks: small signals that add up*: a short, deliberate burst of movement, like ten squats or a minute up the stairs. Since day 7 you have done one a day. Today we go to two or three, and look at what these small moments actually do for you.\n\nThe reason matters in daily life. Most of us sit for long stretches: at a desk, in the car, on the sofa. Finding a free hour to exercise is hard. Finding two minutes, three times a day, is much easier. And your body notices.\n\n## For your heart\n\nResearchers define an exercise snack as a very short bout of brisk effort, often a minute or less, repeated through the day. Climbing stairs briskly is the classic example. A review by researchers who have led much of this work suggests these snacks are a practical, well-tolerated way to build fitness in people who do not exercise.\n\nPooled trials back this up. A 2026 meta-analysis of 21 randomised trials found that exercise snacks improved cardiorespiratory fitness (how well your heart and lungs supply your body during effort), one of the strongest markers of long-term health.\n\nLarge population data point the same way. In a UK study of more than 25,000 adults who did no formal exercise, those who had about three short bursts of vigorous everyday activity a day, each lasting one or two minutes (hurrying up stairs, carrying shopping briskly), had a markedly lower risk of early death over seven years. This kind of study shows a link, not proof. But it is encouraging: everyday bursts seem to count.\n\n## For your blood sugar\n\nLong, unbroken sitting lets blood sugar rise higher and stay up longer after a meal. Breaking it up helps. A meta-analysis of trials comparing uninterrupted sitting with short, frequent breaks found that light walking breaks lowered the rise in blood sugar and insulin after meals. Even standing breaks helped a little, though less.\n\nThis is why your movement moment can be as light as a walk. You do not need to get out of breath to help your blood sugar. Moving the big muscles of your legs is what does the work.\n\n## For energy, focus and mood\n\nThis is where many people feel the difference first. A short burst gets blood moving, wakes you up and gives your mind a break from the screen. Research on mood, fatigue and focus after exercise snacks is promising but still early, so we will not promise more than that. What we can say is that many people come back to their desk clearer and in a better mood than when they left it.\n\n## Why 2 to 3 a day\n\nOne snack is a good start. Two or three spread across the day break up more of your sitting time, give you more chances to steady your blood sugar after meals, and add up to more total movement without ever feeling like a workout. They also build the habit faster: the more often you do something small, the more automatic it becomes.\n\nEasier is always fine. A walk counts. The aim is to move, often, not to push hard.\n\n## What to do\n\nYour habit from today: **2 to 3 movement moments a day**. It can be as light as a walk.\n\n- Choose a movement: it can be as light as a walk.\n- Or a few easy moves: squats, calf raises, a chest opener.\n- Push-ups too, if you're comfortable.\n\nGood moments to use: after a meal, mid-morning, or whenever you have been sitting for a while. Stop if anything hurts. If you have a heart condition, high blood pressure or a joint problem, ask your doctor or your nutritionist before adding brisk efforts.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Clarity of mind, especially after a long stretch at a screen.\n- Stable blood sugar, felt as fewer dips and cravings after meals.\n- More energy, without reaching for coffee.\n- A stronger body overall, as the small moves add up over the weeks.\n\n**Two or three short movement moments a day add up to real benefits for your heart, your blood sugar and your mood.**\n\n## References\n\n- Exercise Snacks: A Novel Strategy to Improve Cardiometabolic Health. Islam H et al. *Exercise and Sport Sciences Reviews*, 2022. PMID: 34669625. DOI: [10.1249/JES.0000000000000275](https://doi.org/10.1249/JES.0000000000000275)\n- Effects of exercise snacks on cardiorespiratory fitness, body composition, and blood lipids in adults across different age groups: a systematic review and meta-analysis. Meng L et al. *Journal of Nutrition, Health and Aging*, 2026. PMID: 42571752. DOI: [10.1016/j.jnha.2026.100940](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.jnha.2026.100940)\n- Association of wearable device-measured vigorous intermittent lifestyle physical activity with mortality. Stamatakis E et al. *Nature Medicine*, 2022. PMID: 36482104. DOI: [10.1038/s41591-022-02100-x](https://doi.org/10.1038/s41591-022-02100-x)\n- The Acute Effects of Interrupting Prolonged Sitting Time in Adults with Standing and Light-Intensity Walking on Biomarkers of Cardiometabolic Health in Adults: A Systematic Review and Meta-analysis. Buffey AJ et al. *Sports Medicine*, 2022. PMID: 35147898. DOI: [10.1007/s40279-022-01649-4](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40279-022-01649-4)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/mouvement"]},{"slug":"nsdr-explained","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_nsdr_explained","title":"NSDR, explained","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 10","summary":"NSDR, or non-sleep deep rest, is a guided way to let your body and mind settle deeply while you stay awake; it is a useful wind-down, not a replacement for sleep.","body":"*NSDR is a guided way to rest deeply while you stay awake: a reset for your nervous system, not a substitute for sleep.*\n\nSome evenings your body is tired but your mind will not stop. You lie down and the day replays itself: the email you forgot, tomorrow's meeting. Or in the middle of the afternoon you feel flat, but a nap would leave you groggy. NSDR is made for those moments. It gives your body a structured way to come down from \"on\" without having to fall asleep.\n\n## What NSDR is\n\nNSDR stands for *non-sleep deep rest*. It is a recent umbrella name for guided practices that help you relax deeply while staying awake, or close to it. The best known of these is **yoga nidra**, a traditional practice whose name means roughly \"yogic sleep\".\n\nA session is simple. You lie on your back, close your eyes and follow a voice. It guides you to breathe slowly, then to move your attention through your body, part by part: your feet, your legs, your hands, your face. Sometimes it adds a picture to rest your mind on. You do not need to be flexible, and you do not need any experience with meditation. Your only job is to listen.\n\n## How it differs from sleep\n\nIn sleep, you lose awareness and your brain moves through its own cycles across the night. These cycles do essential work for your memory, your mood, your hormones and your recovery, and nothing else does that work for you.\n\nIn NSDR, you stay aware, or drift at the edge of sleep. You may feel heavy, warm and very still. It can leave you feeling rested, but it **does not replace sleep**. Think of it as a way to help you towards sleep in the evening, or to recover a little after a poor night, not as a way to need less of it.\n\n## How it differs from meditation\n\nMany forms of meditation ask you to sit up and hold your attention on something, like your breath, and to bring it back each time it wanders. That takes some effort, and many beginners find it frustrating.\n\nNSDR asks for less. You lie down, the voice does the steering, and the aim is rest rather than concentration. If your mind wanders, you simply come back to the next instruction. If you fall asleep, that is fine too, especially in the evening.\n\n## What the evidence shows, and what it doesn't\n\nThe research on yoga nidra is growing and encouraging, but much of it is still small or of modest quality. A 2025 review that pooled 73 studies with over 5,000 people found improvements in stress, anxiety and low mood. The authors were clear that the studies were often weak, and that the true effects are probably smaller than the numbers suggest. A review of six randomised trials on sleep found improvements in how quickly people fell asleep and how well they slept, again with a warning about study quality.\n\nOne of the better trials is reassuring for short sessions. Adults who practised an 11-minute online yoga nidra, ideally every day for two months, had small improvements in well-being compared with people on a waiting list.\n\nSo the honest summary: NSDR is a low-cost, low-risk way to wind down that may help stress and sleep. The effects are modest, and it works best as a regular habit, not a one-off fix.\n\n## When to use it\n\n- In the evening, as your 10 minutes to wind down.\n- After a poor night, as a midday reset.\n- When you feel wired but tired.\n- After a demanding day, physically or mentally.\n\nTo start, try the 10-minute guided NSDR from Huberman Lab on YouTube: [10-minute NSDR](https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=KHIbgSN2qAU). Start it, then turn your phone face down, so the screen does not pull you back in. Headphones help.\n\n## What to do\n\nYour habit: **10 minutes to wind down: box breathing or NSDR**.\n\n- Box breathing: in 4, hold 4, out 4, hold 4.\n- Or a guided NSDR, lying down.\n- Lights low.\n\nDo it lying somewhere safe and comfortable, never while driving. If lying still with your thoughts brings up something distressing, open your eyes and stop; it is fine to choose box breathing instead.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Falling asleep faster on the evenings they use it.\n- A calmer mind, with less of the day replaying.\n- Better recovery: waking a little more rested, or feeling restored after a midday session.\n\n**NSDR is ten minutes of guided deep rest that helps you wind down; let it lead you into sleep, never replace it.**\n\n## References\n\n- Effects of Yoga Nidra on Stress, Anxiety, and Depression: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. Ghai S et al. *Annals of the New York Academy of Sciences*, 2025. PMID: 41327816. DOI: [10.1111/nyas.70149](https://doi.org/10.1111/nyas.70149)\n- Efficacy of Yoga Nidra in Managing Sleep Disorders: A Systematic Review of Randomized Controlled Trials. Dutta A et al. *Journal of Integrative and Complementary Medicine*, 2025. PMID: 41144325. DOI: [10.1177/27683605251390728](https://doi.org/10.1177/27683605251390728)\n- The Effects of an Online Yoga Nidra Meditation on Subjective Well-Being and Diurnal Salivary Cortisol: A Randomised Controlled Trial. Moszeik EN et al. *Stress and Health*, 2025. PMID: 40373021. DOI: [10.1002/smi.70049](https://doi.org/10.1002/smi.70049)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/sommeil"]},{"slug":"your-evening-routine","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_your_evening_routine","title":"Your evening routine","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 10","summary":"Doing the same few steps in the same order each evening tells your body that sleep is coming, and three simple steps make the whole routine.","body":"*The same few steps, in the same order, each evening tell your body that sleep is on its way.*\n\nThink of how a child gets ready for bed: bath, pyjamas, story, lights out. Same steps, same order, most nights. It works for adults too. Your body cannot switch from a busy day to deep sleep in a minute. It needs a way down, and a routine is that way down.\n\nSince day 4 you have been following the 3-2-1 rule. Today you add two steps, so your evening becomes a simple routine in three parts.\n\n## Why the same steps help\n\nYour body runs on a clock, and that clock likes regular signals. Light, food, activity and timing all tell it what time of day it is. When your evenings follow the same pattern, your body learns to expect sleep, and starts to get ready for it a little earlier: winding down, feeling drowsy at the right moment.\n\nThere is a second effect, in your mind. A routine you repeat becomes a cue. After a couple of weeks, simply opening your book or lying down to breathe starts to say \"the day is over\". You stop deciding what to do each evening, and decisions are one of the things that keep a busy mind awake.\n\nTo be honest about the evidence: each piece of good sleep habits has been linked with better sleep, but researchers have rarely tested a full routine as one package in the general public. So treat this as a sensible, low-risk experiment, and use your evening check-in to see how it works for you.\n\n## Part 1: the 3-2-1 rule\n\nYou know this one (see *The 3-2-1 rule*). Three hours before bed, your last food of the day, so digestion is not working late. Two hours before, no hard exercise: a review of the research found that evening exercise does not usually harm sleep, but hard efforts ending within about an hour of bedtime can make it harder to fall asleep. Two hours gives a comfortable margin. Stretching, light movement and a walk are fine. One hour before, no scrolling.\n\n## Part 2: a book instead of a screen\n\nThe last hour is where a book earns its place. In a careful sleep-lab study, people who read on a light-emitting tablet before bed took longer to fall asleep, made less melatonin (the hormone that helps signal night-time), had their body clock pushed later, and were less alert the next morning than when they read a printed book. Same reading, different light.\n\nA book also gives your mind something calm to do. A story or a gentle subject pulls attention away from the day, without the pull of notifications.\n\n## Part 3: 10 minutes to wind down\n\nThe last step is a deliberate slowing down, for about ten minutes. You have two options.\n\n**Box breathing.** Breathe in for 4, hold for 4, breathe out for 4, hold for 4, and repeat. The even rhythm gives your mind one simple thing to follow. In a month-long randomised study, five minutes a day of structured breathing, box breathing among the options, improved mood. The version with long, slow breaths out did best, so if the holds feel tight, let your breath out be a little longer.\n\n**An NSDR.** Non-sleep deep rest is a guided practice you do lying down, letting your body settle while you stay awake or half-awake. It has its own article: *NSDR, explained*.\n\n## What to do\n\nYour evening, in three steps.\n\n**The 3-2-1 rule: 3 h no food, 2 h no intense exercise, 1 h no screens**\n- 3 hours before bed: your last food of the day.\n- 2 hours before: no hard exercise. Stretching, light movement and a walk are fine.\n- 1 hour before: no scrolling. Answering a message is fine; it's the stimulating stuff you leave.\n\n**A book instead of a screen**\n- Choose tonight's book now.\n- Paper, or an e-reader without the lights up.\n- Even 10 pages.\n\n**10 minutes to wind down: box breathing or NSDR**\n- Box breathing: in 4, hold 4, out 4, hold 4.\n- Or a guided NSDR, lying down.\n- Lights low.\n\nDo the breathing sitting or lying down, never in water or while driving. If a hold feels uncomfortable, shorten it.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Falling asleep faster.\n- Waking less in the night.\n- Calmer evenings, with less noise in your head.\n- Feeling sleepier at the right time.\n- Better recovery, and a little more energy the next morning.\n\n**A short routine, repeated every evening, teaches your body when it is time to sleep.**\n\n## References\n\n- The role of sleep hygiene in promoting public health: A review of empirical evidence. Irish LA et al. *Sleep Medicine Reviews*, 2015. PMID: 25454674. DOI: [10.1016/j.smrv.2014.10.001](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.smrv.2014.10.001)\n- Effects of Evening Exercise on Sleep in Healthy Participants: A Systematic Review and Meta-Analysis. Stutz J et al. *Sports Medicine*, 2019. PMID: 30374942. DOI: [10.1007/s40279-018-1015-0](https://doi.org/10.1007/s40279-018-1015-0)\n- Evening use of light-emitting eReaders negatively affects sleep, circadian timing, and next-morning alertness. Chang AM et al. *Proceedings of the National Academy of Sciences of the USA*, 2015. PMID: 25535358. DOI: [10.1073/pnas.1418490112](https://doi.org/10.1073/pnas.1418490112)\n- Brief structured respiration practices enhance mood and reduce physiological arousal. Balban MY et al. *Cell Reports Medicine*, 2023. PMID: 36630953. DOI: [10.1016/j.xcrm.2022.100895](https://doi.org/10.1016/j.xcrm.2022.100895)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/sommeil"]},{"slug":"connection-is-health","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_connection_is_health","title":"Connection is health","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 11","summary":"The people in your life are part of your health, and one small message or call a day is a simple way to look after those connections.","body":"*The people in your life are part of your health, as much as what you eat and how you sleep.*\n\nWhen we talk about health, we usually mean food, movement and sleep. Those matter. But think about how you feel after a good conversation with a friend, or how a hard week gets lighter when someone checks in on you. Connection is not a nice extra on top of health. It is one of its foundations.\n\nThe trouble is that, in busy lives, it is often the first thing to slip. Not because we stop caring, but because there is never a \"right moment\" to get in touch. Today's habit makes that moment small and easy.\n\n## Why people matter for health\n\nResearchers have followed hundreds of thousands of people over many years to see how relationships relate to health. A landmark review pooled 148 such studies, with more than 300,000 people in total. Those with stronger social relationships were about 50% more likely to survive over the study periods than those with weaker ones. The authors concluded that the size of this effect is comparable with well-known risk factors for early death.\n\nThe heart seems to be part of the story. A review of long-term studies found that poor social relationships were linked with a 29% higher risk of heart disease and a 32% higher risk of stroke.\n\nIt is important to read these numbers carefully. They come from studies that show a link, not proof of cause, and many things travel together with loneliness, like illness, stress or money worries. But the link is strong and keeps showing up across countries, ages and types of study.\n\n## How connection might help\n\nResearchers are still working out exactly why. A few explanations are likely to play a part:\n\n- **Stress has somewhere to go.** Sharing a worry with someone you trust can make it feel more manageable, and your body's stress response may settle sooner.\n- **People help us look after ourselves.** Friends and family nudge us to eat, rest, move and see a doctor when something is wrong.\n- **Meaning and mood.** Feeling that you matter to someone, and that someone matters to you, is closely tied to how you feel day to day.\n\nYou do not need a large circle. What seems to matter most is feeling connected, not counting contacts.\n\n## Why \"no reason needed\"\n\nMany of us hesitate to get in touch without a reason. *It's been too long. They're busy. It might be awkward.* Research suggests we get this wrong. In a series of experiments, people who sent a short \"thinking of you\" message or a small gift consistently underestimated how much the other person appreciated it. The gap was biggest when the contact came as a surprise, which is exactly what a message \"for no reason\" is.\n\nOther experiments found that people expected small social moments, like chatting to a stranger on a train, to be less pleasant than keeping to themselves. In fact, those who connected felt better. We tend to underestimate how much others want to hear from us, and how good it will feel.\n\nSo the bar is low. A message saying \"saw this and thought of you\" counts. So does a voice note on your walk, or a two-minute call.\n\n## What to do\n\nYour habit from today: **Reach out to someone you care about**.\n\n- Think of one person.\n- A message, a voice note or a call.\n- No reason needed.\n\nIt can be a friend, a family member, a former colleague, a neighbour. Pairing it with something you already do, like your walk after lunch, makes it easier to remember.\n\nIf you are feeling low or alone most of the time, please tell us or your doctor. Support is part of your care, and you do not have to manage it alone.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- Feeling more connected, even on busy days.\n- A lift in mood, often right after the message is sent, and again when a reply comes.\n- Stronger relationships over time, as small contacts become a habit on both sides.\n\n**One small message or call a day is a simple, real way to look after your health.**\n\n## References\n\n- Social relationships and mortality risk: a meta-analytic review. Holt-Lunstad J et al. *PLoS Medicine*, 2010. PMID: 20668659. DOI: [10.1371/journal.pmed.1000316](https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pmed.1000316)\n- Loneliness and social isolation as risk factors for coronary heart disease and stroke: systematic review and meta-analysis of longitudinal observational studies. Valtorta NK et al. *Heart*, 2016. PMID: 27091846. DOI: [10.1136/heartjnl-2015-308790](https://doi.org/10.1136/heartjnl-2015-308790)\n- The surprise of reaching out: Appreciated more than we think. Liu PJ et al. *Journal of Personality and Social Psychology*, 2023. PMID: 35816566. DOI: [10.1037/pspi0000402](https://doi.org/10.1037/pspi0000402)\n- Mistakenly seeking solitude. Epley N et al. *Journal of Experimental Psychology: General*, 2014. PMID: 25019381. DOI: [10.1037/a0037323](https://doi.org/10.1037/a0037323)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/stress"]},{"slug":"the-habit-of-writing","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_the_habit_of_writing","title":"The habit of writing","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 11","summary":"Five minutes of evening writing, three good things and then whatever is on your mind, helps clear your head before sleep and shows you, over time, what drains you and what helps.","body":"*Five minutes of writing in the evening clears your head for sleep and, over the weeks, shows you your own patterns.*\n\nSince day 5 you have been writing down three good things about your day. Today that habit grows a little. You still start with your three good things. Then you keep the pen moving for a few more minutes and write whatever is on your mind.\n\nWhy? Because the thoughts that keep you awake at night are often the ones you have not put anywhere. Writing gives them a place to go.\n\n## Step one: three good things, still first\n\nThree good things comes from positive psychology. In an early randomised study run online, writing down three things that went well each day, and why, for one week was one of the exercises that lifted happiness and eased low mood, with effects that lasted months after the week ended. Later research has been more mixed, and the effects tend to be modest. But the core idea holds: what you pay attention to grows. Spending a minute looking for the good things trains your attention to notice them (see *Three good things*).\n\nStarting with the good also sets the tone. You go into the rest of your writing from a calmer, steadier place.\n\n## Step two: what's on your mind\n\nThen you write freely. A worry about work. Something someone said. A plan for tomorrow. A feeling you cannot quite name yet. There is no right topic.\n\nThe idea that writing about what is on your mind can help is one of the most studied in psychology. Psychologist James Pennebaker began testing it in the 1980s, asking people to write about their deepest thoughts and feelings in short sessions over a few days. A large review of 146 randomised studies later found a small but real benefit for health and well-being on average. Small is the honest word: writing is not a treatment, and it works better for some people than others. But it is free, it takes five minutes, and it has a long track record.\n\nThere is a practical benefit for sleep too. In a sleep-lab study, young adults who spent five minutes before bed writing a specific to-do list for the coming days fell asleep faster than those who wrote about tasks they had already done. Getting tomorrow out of your head and onto paper seems to let your mind let go of it.\n\n## Clearing your mind, noticing patterns\n\nTwo things happen when you write regularly.\n\n**Your mind clears.** A thought that goes round and round in your head often feels bigger than it is. Written down, it becomes one sentence on a page. You can see it, and often you can see what to do about it, or that it can wait until morning.\n\n**Patterns appear.** You do not need to reread your notes for this. The act of writing makes you name things, and after a week or two you start to notice what keeps coming up: the meeting that always drains you, the walk that always helps, the evenings you sleep better. That is useful information, for you and for your work with us. Your evening check-in shows the same patterns in numbers; your writing shows them in your own words.\n\n## What to do\n\nYour habit from today: **5 minutes of writing: three good things, and what's on your mind**.\n\n- Three good things first.\n- Then whatever is on your mind.\n- No rules, no rereading.\n\nIn your phone or a journal, whatever is easiest. Paper has one advantage: no screen in your last hour (see *Your evening routine*).\n\nWriting can sometimes bring up difficult feelings for a short while; that is normal. If something heavy keeps coming back, please talk to someone you trust, to us or to your doctor.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice:\n\n- A clearer head at night, with fewer thoughts going round in circles.\n- Spotting what drains you and what helps, in your own words.\n- Easier sleep, as tomorrow's to-dos are already on paper.\n\n**Five minutes of writing turns a busy mind into a few lines on a page, and lets you sleep on a good thought.**\n\n## References\n\n- Positive psychology progress: empirical validation of interventions. Seligman MEP et al. *American Psychologist*, 2005. PMID: 16045394. DOI: [10.1037/0003-066X.60.5.410](https://doi.org/10.1037/0003-066X.60.5.410)\n- Confronting a traumatic event: toward an understanding of inhibition and disease. Pennebaker JW et al. *Journal of Abnormal Psychology*, 1986. PMID: 3745650. DOI: [10.1037//0021-843x.95.3.274](https://doi.org/10.1037//0021-843x.95.3.274)\n- Experimental disclosure and its moderators: a meta-analysis. Frattaroli J. *Psychological Bulletin*, 2006. PMID: 17073523. DOI: [10.1037/0033-2909.132.6.823](https://doi.org/10.1037/0033-2909.132.6.823)\n- The effects of bedtime writing on difficulty falling asleep: A polysomnographic study comparing to-do lists and completed activity lists. Scullin MK et al. *Journal of Experimental Psychology: General*, 2018. PMID: 29058942. DOI: [10.1037/xge0000374](https://doi.org/10.1037/xge0000374)","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/stress"]},{"slug":"sitting-less","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_sitting_less","title":"Sitting less","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 12","summary":"Long, unbroken sitting quietly slows your body down, and a 10-minute movement break every two hours is one of the easiest ways to undo it.","body":"Think about a normal day. Breakfast sitting down, then the car, train or desk. Lunch at a table. More desk. The sofa in the evening. Many of us sit for most of our waking hours without ever deciding to. It doesn't feel like anything is happening, and that's the point: very little is.\n\n## What long sitting does\n\nWhen you sit for a long stretch, the big muscles in your legs and hips go almost silent. Those are the muscles that, when they work, help pull sugar and fats out of your blood after a meal. Switch them off for hours and the body handles food less efficiently. Blood flow in the legs slows down. Hips and hamstrings stay in one shortened position, and the upper back slowly rounds towards the screen.\n\nOver years, the pattern adds up. A large review of more than a million people found that, even after accounting for exercise, those who sat for the longest each day had a higher risk of type 2 diabetes and of dying from heart disease. The risk seemed to rise most once total sitting went beyond roughly six to eight hours a day. These are observational studies, so they show an association, not proof that sitting alone is the cause. But the message is consistent: a workout in the morning is very good, and it doesn't fully cancel a day spent in a chair.\n\nIn the short term, most people feel it too: stiffness when they stand up, a heavy afternoon, a foggy head.\n\n## Breaking it up is what helps\n\nThe good news is that you don't have to sit less overall to start feeling a difference. *Breaking up* your sitting matters on its own.\n\nIn controlled studies, people who interrupted long periods of sitting with short bouts of light activity had a smaller rise in blood sugar and insulin after meals than when they sat straight through. Light walking worked better than standing alone, and moderate movement worked a little better again. Most of these studies used breaks of a few minutes every 20 to 30 minutes. Our habit starts with a rhythm that is easier to keep, ten minutes every two hours. If you find you can stand up more often than that, even better.\n\nReminders help. In workplace studies, simple computer prompts reduced the number of long, unbroken sitting bouts. That is why the last step of today's habit is a quiet reminder: you shouldn't need willpower to remember.\n\n## Moving through range\n\nA movement break is also a chance to take your joints through the range that sitting takes away. You don't need a stretching routine. Stand up, reach your arms overhead, open your chest, rock gently into your hips, roll your shoulders, take a few slow steps or a few slow squats. The aim is not to force flexibility. It is to remind your hips, back and shoulders that they can move, under control and without pain, so they feel looser when you sit back down.\n\n**Safety:** move slowly and never into pain. If you have a back, hip or knee problem, or a medical condition, check with your doctor or nutritionist about what suits you.\n\n## What to do\n\n**Move 10 minutes every 2 hours.** Every 2 hours, stand up and move for 10 minutes.\n\n- **A walk if you can.** Around the block, down the corridor, to the kitchen and back the long way.\n- **Stairs, a stretch, a call on your feet.** Climb a floor or two, open your chest and hips, or take your next call standing or walking.\n- **Set a quiet reminder.** A gentle alarm every two hours on your phone or watch, so the break finds you.\n\n## What to look for\n\nMany people notice, often within a few days:\n\n- **Less stiffness**, especially when you stand up after a long stretch.\n- **Sharper focus after the break**, rather than a slow fade through the afternoon.\n- **More steps without trying**, because the breaks add up across the day.\n\nYou may also see it in your evening check-in, in steadier energy across the afternoon.\n\n**You don't have to give up your chair, just get out of it: ten minutes, every two hours.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Patterson R, McNamara E, Tainio M, et al. Sedentary behaviour and risk of all-cause, cardiovascular and cancer mortality, and incident type 2 diabetes: a systematic review and dose response meta-analysis. *European Journal of Epidemiology*. 2018;33(9):811-829. PMID: 29589226. doi:10.1007/s10654-018-0380-1\n2. Buffey AJ, Herring MP, Langley CK, et al. The acute effects of interrupting prolonged sitting time in adults with standing and light-intensity walking on biomarkers of cardiometabolic health in adults: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *Sports Medicine*. 2022;52(8):1765-1787. PMID: 35147898. doi:10.1007/s40279-022-01649-4\n3. Quan M, Xun P, Wu H, et al. Effects of interrupting prolonged sitting on postprandial glycemia and insulin responses: a network meta-analysis. *Journal of Sport and Health Science*. 2021;10(4):419-429. PMID: 33359636. doi:10.1016/j.jshs.2020.12.006\n4. Shrestha N, Kukkonen-Harjula KT, Verbeek JH, et al. Workplace interventions for reducing sitting at work. *Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews*. 2018;12(12):CD010912. PMID: 30556590. doi:10.1002/14651858.CD010912.pub5","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/mouvement"]},{"slug":"the-power-of-walking","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_the_power_of_walking","title":"The power of walking","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 12","summary":"Walking is the simplest habit you have, and one of the most underrated: it helps your heart, your blood sugar, your mood and your thinking, and ten minutes every two hours is enough to start.","body":"Most of us think of walking as getting from one place to another. It rarely counts as \"exercise\" in our heads, so we don't give it much credit. But walking is the movement your body was built for. It needs no kit, no changing room and no plan. You can do it in work clothes, on a phone call, in the rain. And because it asks so little, it is one of the few habits people actually keep.\n\nToday we build on the walks you already take after meals. The new habit is **Move 10 minutes every 2 hours**: every two hours, stand up and move for ten minutes, ideally on your feet and walking.\n\n## Your heart likes it\n\nYou don't need to run to give your heart a reason to adapt. When researchers pooled studies of outdoor walking groups, people who walked regularly saw small but real improvements: lower blood pressure, a lower resting heart rate, better fitness and less body fat. No notable side effects were reported.\n\nLarge reviews of step counts point the same way. People who take more steps each day tend to have a lower risk of heart and circulation problems and tend to live longer, and the benefit seems to start with modest increases, not only at big targets. These are observational studies, so they show a link rather than proof, but the pattern is consistent.\n\n## It steadies your blood sugar\n\nYou have already met this on day 2 with the walk after eating. When your leg muscles work, they draw glucose out of the blood and use it, a bit like a sponge soaking up water. Sitting still for hours switches that sponge off.\n\nIn controlled studies, breaking up long sitting with short, light walks lowered the rise in blood sugar and insulin after meals, compared with sitting straight through. Walking did this better than simply standing. You don't feel your blood sugar directly, but many people notice it as steadier energy and less of the heavy, sleepy feeling in the afternoon.\n\n## Your mood and your ideas come with you\n\nWalking is good for the head as well as the body. In the walking-group studies above, people also reported lower depression scores.\n\nIt also helps you think. In a set of experiments at Stanford, people came up with more creative ideas while walking than while sitting, and the effect lasted a little while after they sat back down. Walking outside gave the freshest ideas of all. So if you're stuck on a problem, a ten-minute walk is not time lost. It may be the most useful ten minutes of your afternoon.\n\n## Why ten minutes every two hours\n\nTen minutes is long enough to get the blood moving, loosen your hips and back, and clear your head. Every two hours is often enough to stop a whole morning or afternoon disappearing into a chair, and realistic enough to fit around work. You will not always manage it, and that's fine. Each break counts on its own.\n\nIf walking isn't possible, any movement is better than none: stairs, a few stretches, or simply standing up for a phone call. The habit is about getting off the chair, not about the perfect walk.\n\n**Safety:** go at a pace that feels easy to moderate. If you have pain, a heart condition or a recent injury, check with your doctor or nutritionist first, and stop if anything hurts.\n\n## What to do\n\n**Move 10 minutes every 2 hours.** Every 2 hours, stand up and move for 10 minutes.\n\n- **A walk if you can.** Outside is best; a corridor or a loop of the office works too.\n- **Stairs, a stretch, a call on your feet.** If you can't walk, move another way. Take your next call standing or walking.\n- **Set a quiet reminder.** A gentle alarm on your phone or watch, every two hours, so you don't have to remember.\n\n## What to look for\n\nOver the next days, many people notice:\n\n- **Less stiffness**, especially in the hips, lower back and neck.\n- **Sharper focus after the break**, and sometimes a better idea than the one you sat down with.\n- **More steps without trying**, which you may see on your phone or watch.\n\nKeep an eye on your evening check-in too: energy and mood are often the first curves to move.\n\n**Walking is the habit that asks the least and gives back the most: ten minutes, every two hours, is enough.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Hanson S, Jones A. Is there evidence that walking groups have health benefits? A systematic review and meta-analysis. *British Journal of Sports Medicine*. 2015;49(11):710-715. PMID: 25601182. doi:10.1136/bjsports-2014-094157\n2. Xu C, Jia J, Zhao B, et al. Objectively measured daily steps and health outcomes: an umbrella review of the systematic review and meta-analysis of observational studies. *BMJ Open*. 2024;14(10):e088524. PMID: 39384238. doi:10.1136/bmjopen-2024-088524\n3. Buffey AJ, Herring MP, Langley CK, et al. The acute effects of interrupting prolonged sitting time in adults with standing and light-intensity walking on biomarkers of cardiometabolic health in adults: a systematic review and meta-analysis. *Sports Medicine*. 2022;52(8):1765-1787. PMID: 35147898. doi:10.1007/s40279-022-01649-4\n4. Oppezzo M, Schwartz DL. Give your ideas some legs: the positive effect of walking on creative thinking. *Journal of Experimental Psychology: Learning, Memory, and Cognition*. 2014;40(4):1142-1152. PMID: 24749966. doi:10.1037/a0036577","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4","pillar/mouvement"]},{"slug":"habits-that-last","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_habits_that_last","title":"Habits that last","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 13","summary":"The habits that stay with you are the ones you would miss, not the ones you think you should do, so today you choose a few to keep.","body":"Over the last twelve days you have tried a lot: meal photos, the evening check-in, walks after eating, morning movement, daylight, breathing, writing, a calmer evening. That was the point. Two weeks is a chance to taste each habit and see what it does for *you*. Today there is nothing new to add. Instead, you look back and decide what comes with you.\n\n## Your body responds to what you repeat\n\nYour body is always responding to signals: food, movement, light, sleep, stress, breathing. But it doesn't respond to what you *intend* to do. It responds to what you repeatedly expose it to. A walk once is pleasant. A walk most days, for months, is a signal your body can adapt to.\n\nThat is why the habits you keep matter more than the habits you tried. A short list you live by beats a long list you abandon in March.\n\n## Two weeks is a start, not the finish\n\nHabits take time to become automatic, the point where you do them without having to think. In a review of studies on health habits, the typical time was around two months, and it varied enormously from person to person: some people got there in days, others took most of a year. So if nothing feels automatic yet, nothing has gone wrong. You are early.\n\nWhat helps a habit settle is simple: doing the same thing, in the same situation, again and again. The situation becomes the cue. Coffee in the kitchen, and your head goes out of the window. Lunch finished, and your feet head for the door. Lights low, and the book comes out. The more often the cue and the action meet, the less effort it takes.\n\n## Keep the ones you'd miss\n\nHere is the question we suggest you ask of each habit: *If I stopped this tomorrow, would I miss it?*\n\nNot \"is this good for me?\" They all are. Not \"should I be doing this?\" That question keeps habits on a list, not in your life. The habits that last tend to be the ones that feel good while you do them, or the ones whose effect you can feel: the calmer evening, the lighter digestion, the clearer head after a walk. Research on exercise points the same way: how people feel *during* activity helps predict whether they keep doing it. And in the habit studies, habits that people chose for themselves, and habits done in the morning, tended to become stronger.\n\nSo be honest. If the breathing made your day, keep it. If the writing felt like homework, let it go for now, without guilt. You can come back to anything later.\n\n## Small and anchored\n\nKeep it to a few, the ones that earned their place. Give each one a fixed moment in your day, linked to something you already do. Keep the easy version: a five-minute walk you actually take is worth more than a twenty-minute walk you plan. And if you miss a day, start again the next. Missing once doesn't undo a habit; what builds it is repetition over weeks, not a perfect streak.\n\n## What to do\n\n- **Look back at your two weeks.** Open your Trends and see which curves moved, and when.\n- **Ask of each habit: would I miss it?** Keep the ones where the honest answer is yes.\n- **Choose a few to keep.** Let the others rest. They are not gone; you can bring any of them back later.\n- **Anchor each one** to a moment you already have: waking up, a meal, the end of work, bedtime.\n\n## What to look for\n\nAs you carry your chosen habits forward, many people notice:\n\n- **Less effort** each week: the habit starts to happen on its own.\n- **A day that feels like yours**, built on things you enjoy rather than rules.\n- **Clearer signals** in your check-in, because you can see what each habit is doing.\n\nYou may also find you're more patient with yourself on the days you miss.\n\n**Keep the habits you'd miss, anchor them to your day, and give them time: that is how they last.**\n\n## References\n\n1. Singh B, Murphy A, Maher C, Smith AE. Time to form a habit: a systematic review and meta-analysis of health behaviour habit formation and its determinants. *Healthcare (Basel)*. 2024;12(23):2488. PMID: 39685110. doi:10.3390/healthcare12232488\n2. Gardner B, Lally P, Wardle J. Making health habitual: the psychology of 'habit-formation' and general practice. *British Journal of General Practice*. 2012;62(605):664-666. PMID: 23211256. doi:10.3399/bjgp12X659466\n3. Rhodes RE, Kates A. Can the affective response to exercise predict future motives and physical activity behavior? A systematic review of published evidence. *Annals of Behavioral Medicine*. 2015;49(5):715-731. PMID: 25921307. doi:10.1007/s12160-015-9704-5","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4"]},{"slug":"what-a-personal-programme-adds","path":"patient_resources.foundation_track_what_a_personal_programme_adds","title":"What a personal programme adds","kicker":"Foundation Track · Day 14","summary":"The app gives everyone the same strong foundations; a personal programme starts from you, with a plan made for you, labs where they are useful, and follow-up with Thomas.","body":"Two weeks ago you started from where you were. Since then you have logged meals, checked in each evening, walked, breathed, moved and slept a little differently. You have chosen the habits you want to keep. Whatever you decide next, that work is yours, and it doesn't stop today.\n\nThis article is about one option for what comes after: a personal programme. It is not the only good path, and we want to be clear about what it adds, and what it doesn't.\n\n## What the app does well\n\nThe Foundation Track is the same for everyone, on purpose. Daylight, movement, meals with protein and fibre, a calmer evening, a moment to breathe: these basics help almost everyone, and most people feel a difference from them alone. Your check-ins and meal photos have already started to show *your* patterns in Trends.\n\nIf that is enough for you right now, you can simply keep going with the app. Keep your habits, keep checking in, and keep reading. That is a complete choice, not a lesser one.\n\n## What a personal programme adds\n\nWhat the app can't do is look at you as a whole person and decide what *you* need next. That is where a personal programme comes in.\n\n- **A plan made for you.** Not the general basics, but the few changes that matter most for your body, your goals and your life: your food, your movement, your sleep, your stress, in an order that makes sense.\n- **Labs, where they are useful.** Sometimes the most helpful next step is to measure rather than guess. When tests would genuinely change the plan, Thomas will suggest them and explain why. When they wouldn't, he won't.\n- **A programme built on your own data.** Your check-ins, your meal photos, your history and any results, read together, so the plan follows what is actually happening for you rather than what happens on average.\n- **Follow-up with Thomas.** Regular check-ins with a practitioner who knows your story, to adjust as you go: what's working, what isn't, what to change next.\n\nA good plan works like recovery after training: your body can only rebuild with the right materials, in the right amounts, at the right time. A personal programme is about finding *your* right materials, rather than the general ones.\n\nA programme doesn't replace your doctor, and it doesn't promise a particular result. What it offers is attention: someone looking closely at your patterns and helping you act on them.\n\n## The 20-minute review call\n\nTo help you decide, you can book a 20-minute review call with Thomas. It is a conversation, not a sales pitch. Together you look at your two weeks: what you noticed, which habits you kept, and what your check-ins show. You can talk about what you would like to change next, and Thomas will tell you honestly whether a personal programme would help, or whether the app is enough for now.\n\nThere is no obligation either way. Plenty of people take the call and carry on with the app, with a clearer idea of where to focus.\n\n## What to do\n\n- **Look back at your two weeks** in Trends, and note one or two things you'd like to understand better.\n- **Book your 20-minute review call with Thomas**, if you'd like to talk it through.\n- **Bring your questions**: what you noticed, what you kept, what you still want to change.\n- **Or simply keep going** with the habits you chose. The app stays with you.\n\n## What to look for\n\nA personal programme may be worth a conversation if you notice:\n\n- **Something that hasn't shifted**, even with the basics in place: energy, sleep, digestion or mood.\n- **Questions the app can't answer**, such as *why* your body responds the way it does.\n- **A wish for guidance**, someone to help you choose the next step and adjust it with you.\n\nIf none of these apply, that is good news too: your foundations are doing their job.\n\n**Whatever you choose, the foundations you built in these two weeks are yours to keep; a personal programme simply builds the next floor around you.**\n\n## References\n\nNone. This article describes how the practice works; it makes no health claims that need a source.","tags":["format/article","member/foundation-track","member/minutes:4"]}]$fa$; r jsonb; n uuid; ch uuid; c int; tg text; slugs text[];
begin
  select id into ch from public.delivery_channels where slug = 'patient_app';
  if ch is null then raise exception 'no patient_app channel'; end if;
  select array_agg(e->>'slug') into slugs from jsonb_array_elements(d) e;
  for r in select * from jsonb_array_elements(d) loop
    insert into public.kb_nodes (space, path, template, title, slug, kicker, summary, body_md, visibility, status, published_at, editorial_priority, ai_access, language)
      values ('patient_resources', (r->>'path')::ltree, 'article', r->>'title', r->>'slug', r->>'kicker', r->>'summary', r->>'body',
              array['public_free']::public.library_visibility[], 'published', now(), 'recommended', 'internal_only', 'en') returning id into n;
    insert into public.kb_node_channels (node_id, channel_id, enabled) values (n, ch, true);
    for tg in select jsonb_array_elements_text(r->'tags') loop
      insert into public.kb_tags (kind, slug, label) values (split_part(tg, '/', 1), split_part(tg, '/', 2), split_part(tg, '/', 2)) on conflict (kind, slug) do nothing;
      insert into public.kb_node_tags (node_id, tag_id) select n, id from public.kb_tags where kind = split_part(tg, '/', 1) and slug = split_part(tg, '/', 2);
    end loop;
  end loop;
  select count(*) into c from public.content_list(p_channel_slug => 'website', p_allowed_tiers => array['public_free']::public.library_visibility[], p_limit => 500) x where x.slug = any(slugs);
  if c <> 0 then raise exception '% articles would reach the public website', c; end if;
  select count(*) into c from public.content_list(p_channel_slug => 'patient_app', p_allowed_tiers => array['public_free']::public.library_visibility[], p_limit => 500) x where x.slug = any(slugs);
  if c <> 29 then raise exception 'only % of 29 articles reach the app library', c; end if;
  raise notice 'applied: % articles', c;
end
$do$;
